aps that's a good coordinate system.\n\nSo:\n\n- B: (0,0)\n\n- C: (a, 0)\n\n- A: (p, q)\n\nThen, side AC has length 1: distance between (p, q) and (a, 0) is sqrt[(p - a)^2 + q^2] = 1.\n\nSide AB has length c: sqrt[(p)^2 + q^2] = c.\n\nSide BC is length a: distance between B and C is a, which is given as BC = a.\n\nBut the problem states that side b =1. Wait, side b is opposite angle B. In standard notation, side b is AC, since angle B is at vertex B, which is opposite side AC. Wait, no. Wait, angle B is at vertex B, so the side opposite angle B is AC, which is side b. Therefore, in this coordinate system, AC is side b =1. Therefore, distance from A to C is 1. So sqrt[(p - a)^2 + q^2] = 1.\n\nAlso, side AB is length c: sqrt(p\u00b2 + q\u00b2) = c.\n\nSide BC is length a: sqrt[(a - 0)^2 + 0] = a.\n\nThen, we can also compute angles using coordinates. For example, angle at B is angle between BA and BC. The vectors BA = (p, q) and BC = (a, 0). So the angle B can be found using the dot product:\n\ncos B = (BA \u00b7 BC) / (|BA| |BC|) = (p * a + q * 0) / (c * a) ) = (pa) / (a c) ) = p / c.\n\nSimilarly, angle at C: the vectors CB = (-a, 0) and CA = (p - a, q). So cos C = (CB \u00b7 CA) / (|CB| |CA|) = [(-a)(p - a) + 0 * q] / (a * 1) ) = [-a(p - a)] / a ) = -(p - a) = a - p.\n\nSo we have:\n\ncos B = p / c,\n\ncos C = a - p.\n\nGiven that, the original equation given in the problem is cos C + (2a + c) cos B = 0.\n\nSubstituting the expressions for cos B and cos C:\n\n(a - p) + (2a + c)(p / c) = 0.\n\nMultiply through:\n\n(a - p) + (2a + c)(p / c) = 0.\n\nLet's expand the second term:\n\n(2a p)/c + (c p)/c = (2a p)/c + p.\n\nSo the equation becomes:\n\n(a - p) + (2a p)/c + p = 0.\n\nSimplify:\n\na - p + 2a p / c + p = a + 2a p / c = 0.\n\nSo:\n\na + (2a p)/c = 0.\n\nFactor out a:\n\na [1 + (2p)/c] = 0.\n\nSince a is a side length, a \u2260 0, so 1 + (2p)/c = 0.\n\nTherefore:\n\n(2p)/c = -1 => p = -c/2.\n\nSo from this coordinate system, the x-coordinate of point A is p = -c