ft( \\frac{1}{m + 1}, \\frac{1}{m} \\right] \\), leading to the interval for \\( f(x) \\) as \\( \\left[ \\frac{m^2 + 1}{m(m + 1)}, \\frac{(m + 1)^2 + 1}{(m + 1)^2} \\right) \\).\n\n4. **Key Observations:**\n   - The intervals for \\( f(x) \\) in both Case 1 and Case 3 are the same for each integer \\( n \\) or \\( m \\).\n   - The minimum value of \\( f(x) \\) in these intervals is \\( \\frac{5}{6} \\) (achieved at \\( x = 2 \\), \\( x = \\frac{1}{2} \\), etc.).\n   - The maximum value approaches \\( \\frac{5}{4} \\) but is never attained.\n   - The special case at \\( x = 1 \\) gives \\( f(1, 1) = 0.5 \\).\n\nCombining all these results, the range of the function is the union of the single value \\( 0.5 \\) and the interval from \\( \\frac{5}{6} \\) to \\( \\frac{5}{4} \\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\left\\{\\frac{1}{2}\\right\\} \\cup \\left[ \\frac{5}{6}, \\frac{5}{4} \\right)}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\nGiven the function:\n\\[ \nf(x, y) = \\frac{x + y}{\\lfloor x \\rfloor \\lfloor y \\rfloor + \\lfloor x \\rfloor + \\lfloor y \\rfloor + 1} \n\\] \nwith the constraints \\( x > 0 \\), \\( y > 0 \\), and \\( xy = 1 \\), we are tasked with finding the range of this function.\n\n1. **Case Analysis**:\n\n   **Case 1**: \\( x = 1 \\) and \\( y = 1 \\):\n\n   \\[\n   f(1, 1) = \\frac{1 + 1}{\\lfloor 1 \\rfloor \\lfloor 1 \\rfloor + \\lfloor 1 \\rfloor + \\lfloor 1 \\rfloor + 1} = \\frac{2}{1 \\cdot 1 + 1 + 1 + 1} = \\frac{2}{4} = \\frac{1}{2}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Case 2**: \\( x > 1 \\) and \\( y = \\frac{1}{x} \\):\n\n   Let \\( x = n + \\alpha \\), where \\( n \\geqslant 1 \\), \\( n \\in \\mathbb{N} \\), and \\( 0 \\leqslant \\alpha < 1 \\). Then:\n\n   \\[\n   y = \\frac{1}{n + \\alpha}, \\quad \\lfloor y \\rfloor = 0, \\quad \\lfloor x \\rfloor = n\n   \\]\n\n   Hence,\n\n   \\[\n   f(x, y) = \\frac{n + \\alpha + \\frac{1}{n + \\alpha}}{n \\cdot 0 + n + 0 + 1} = \\frac{n + \\alpha + \\frac{1}{n + \\alpha}}{n + 1}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Prove that \\( y = x + \\frac{1}{x} \\) 