{7\\alpha - 6\\alpha}{2}\\right) = \n   2 \\sin \\left(\\frac{13\\alpha}{2}\\right) \\cos \\left(\\frac{\\alpha}{2}\\right)\n   \\]\n\n3. **Substitute These Back into The Original Expression:**\n\n   Substitute the obtained expressions back into the grouped formula:\n   \\[\n   (\\sin 8\\alpha + \\sin 5\\alpha) - (\\sin 7\\alpha + \\sin 6\\alpha) = \n   2 \\sin \\left(\\frac{13\\alpha}{2}\\right) \\cos \\left(\\frac{3\\alpha}{2}\\right) - 2 \\sin \\left(\\frac{13\\alpha}{2}\\right) \\cos \\left(\\frac{\\alpha}{2}\\right)\n   \\]\n\n   Factor out the common terms:\n   \\[\n   2 \\sin \\left(\\frac{13\\alpha}{2}\\right) \\left( \\cos \\left(\\frac{3\\alpha}{2}\\right) - \\cos \\left(\\frac{\\alpha}{2}\\right) \\right)\n   \\]\n\n4. **Simplify The Difference of Cosines:**\n\n   Use the identity for the difference of cosines:\n   \\[\n   \\cos x - \\cos y = -2 \\sin \\left(\\frac{x + y}{2}\\right) \\sin \\left(\\frac{x - y}{2}\\right)\n   \\]\n\n   Apply this to \\( \\cos \\left(\\frac{3\\alpha}{2}\\right) - \\cos \\left(\\frac{\\alpha}{2}\\right) \\):\n   \\[\n   \\cos \\left(\\frac{3\\alpha}{2}\\right) - \\cos \\left(\\frac{\\alpha}{2}\\right) = -2 \\sin \\left(\\frac{\\frac{3\\alpha}{2} + \\frac{\\alpha}{2}}{2}\\right) \\sin \\left(\\frac{\\frac{3\\alpha}{2} - \\frac{\\alpha}{2}}{2}\\right)\n   \\]\n\n   Simplify the arguments inside the sine functions:\n   \\[\n   \\cos \\left(\\frac{3\\alpha}{2}\\right) - \\cos \\left(\\frac{\\alpha}{2}\\right) = -2 \\sin \\left(\\frac{4\\alpha}{4}\\right) \\sin \\left(\\frac{2\\alpha}{4}\\right) = -2 \\sin \\alpha \\sin \\frac{\\alpha}{2}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Combine All Terms:**\n\n   Substitute back into our previous expressions:\n   \\[\n   2 \\sin \\left(\\frac{13\\alpha}{2}\\right) \\left(-2 \\sin \\alpha \\sin \\frac{\\alpha}{2}\\right) = -4 \\sin \\alpha \\sin \\frac{\\alpha}{2} \\sin \\frac{13\\alpha}{2}\n   \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\n\n   The final simplified expression is:\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{-4 \\sin \\frac{\\alpha}{2} \\sin \\