he scaled equation (2) becomes:\n\n\\(26x^2 - 46.8xy + 20.8y^2 = 15.6\\)\n\nNow subtract equation (1) from this scaled equation:\n\n(26x\u00b2 - 46.8xy + 20.8y\u00b2) - (26x\u00b2 - 42xy + 17y\u00b2) = 15.6 - 10\n\nCalculating the left-hand side:\n\n26x\u00b2 - 46.8xy + 20.8y\u00b2 -26x\u00b2 +42xy -17y\u00b2\n\nSimplify term by term:\n\n26x\u00b2 -26x\u00b2 = 0\n\n-46.8xy +42xy = (-46.8 +42)xy = -4.8xy\n\n20.8y\u00b2 -17y\u00b2 = 3.8y\u00b2\n\nRight-hand side: 15.6 -10 = 5.6\n\nSo we have:\n\n-4.8xy +3.8y\u00b2 = 5.6\n\nHmm, this simplifies to:\n\n3.8y\u00b2 -4.8xy =5.6\n\nThis still has both x and y terms. Maybe I can factor out a y?\n\nLet me write this as:\n\ny(3.8y -4.8x) =5.6\n\nBut decimals are a bit messy. Maybe I should have used fractions instead of decimals. Let me recast the previous step with fractions.\n\nOriginal equation after subtraction:\n\n( -234/5 xy + 104/5 y\u00b2 ) - ( -42xy +17y\u00b2 ) = 78/5 -10\n\nCompute each term:\n\n-234/5 xy +42xy = (-234/5 + 210/5)xy = (-24/5)xy\n\n104/5 y\u00b2 -17y\u00b2 = 104/5 y\u00b2 -85/5 y\u00b2 =19/5 y\u00b2\n\nRight-hand side: 78/5 -50/5 =28/5\n\nSo the equation becomes:\n\n-24/5 xy +19/5 y\u00b2 =28/5\n\nMultiply both sides by 5 to eliminate denominators:\n\n-24xy +19y\u00b2 =28\n\nSo:\n\n19y\u00b2 -24xy =28\n\nHmm, this is better. So equation (3): 19y\u00b2 -24xy =28\n\nNow, perhaps I can express x in terms of y or vice versa.\n\nLet me see. Let's try to solve for x in terms of y.\n\nFrom equation (3):\n\n19y\u00b2 -24xy =28\n\nLet me rearrange:\n\n-24xy =28 -19y\u00b2\n\nDivide both sides by -24y (assuming y \u22600):\n\nx = (19y\u00b2 -28)/(24y)\n\nOkay, so x is expressed in terms of y. Let's write that as:\n\nx = (19y\u00b2 -28)/(24y)  [Equation 4]\n\nNow, substitute this expression for x into one of the original equations to solve for y. Let's choose equation (2), since the coefficients are smaller, which might make computation easier.\n\nEquation (2):10x\u00b2 -18xy +8y\u00b2 =6\n\nSubstitute x from equation (4):\n\nFirst, compute x\u00b2:\n\nx = (19y\u00b2 -28)/(24y) \u21d2 x\u00b2 = (19y\u00b2 -28)^2 / (24y)^2 = (361y\u2074 - 2*19*28 y\u00b2 +784)/ (576y\u00b2)\n\nCompute each term:\n\n10x\u00b2 =10*(361y\u2074 -1064y\u00b2 +784)/(576y\u00b2)\n\n-18xy = -18*(19y\u00b2 -28)/(24y) * y = -18*(19y\u00b2 -28)/24\n\nSimplify: -18/24 = -3/4, so -3/4*(19y\u00b2 -28)\n\n8y\u00b2 remains 8y\u00b2\n\nSo putting it all together:\n\n10*(361y\u2074 -1064y\u00b2 +784)/(576y\u00b2) - (3/4)(19y\u00b2 -28) +8y\u00b2 =6\n\nThis looks complicated. Let's handle each term step by step.\n\nFirst term: 10*(361y\u2074 -1064y\u00b2 +784)/(576y\u00b2)\n\nLet me factor numerator and denominator:\n\nNumerator: 10*(361y\u2074 -1064y\u00b2 +784)\n\nDenominator:576y\u00b2\n\nSo term1 = [10*(361y\u2074 -1064y\u00b2 +784)] / (576y\u00b2)\n\nSecond term: - (3/4)(19y\u00b2 -28) = -57/4 y\u00b2 +21\n\nThird term:8y\u00b2\n\nCombine all terms:\n\n[10*(361y\u2074 -1064y\u00b2 +784)] / (576y\u00b2) -57/4 y\u00b2 +21 +8y\u00b2 =6\n\nSimplify the polynomial terms:\n\n-57/4 y\u00b2 +8y\u00b2 = (-57/4 +32/4)y\u00b2 = (-25/4)y\u00b2\n\nSo equation becomes:\n\n[10*(361y\u2074 -1064y\u00b2 +784)] / (576y\u00b2) -25/4 y\u00b2 +21 =6\n\nSubtract 6 from both sides:\n\n[10*(361y\u2074 -1064y\u00b2 +784)] / (576y\u00b2) -25/4 y\u00b2 +15 =0\n\nThis seems very messy. Maybe I made a mistake earlier. Let me check my steps.\n\nWait, perhaps substituting x in terms of y into equation (2) is leading to a complicated equation. Maybe substituting into equation (1) would be better? Let's try that.\n\nBut equation (1) is:\n\n26x\u00b2 -42xy +17y\u00b2 =10\n\nAgain, substitute x from equation (4):\n\nx = (19y\u00b2 -28)/(24y)\n\nCompute x\u00b2:\n\nAs before, x\u00b2 = (19y\u00b2 -28)^2 / (576y\u00b2)\n\nCompute each term:\n\n26x\u00b2 =26*(361y\u2074 -1064y\u00b2 +784)/(576y\u00b2)\n\n-42xy = -42*(19y\u00b2 -28)/(24y)*y = -42*(19y\u00b2 -28)/24\n\nSimplify: -42/24 = -7/4, so -7/4*(19y\u00b2 -28)\n\n17y\u00b2 remains 17y\u00b2\n\nSo putting into equation (1):\n\n26*(361y\u2074 -1064y\u00b2 +784)/(576y\u00b2) -7/4*(19y\u00b2 -28) +17y\u00b2 =10\n\nAgain, breaking it down:\n\nFirst term:26*(361y\u2074 -1064y\u00b2 +784)/(576y\u00b2)\n\nSecond term: -7/4*(19y\u00b2 -28) = -133/4 y\u00b2 +49\n\nThird term:17y\u00b2\n\nCombine all terms:\n\n[26*(361y\u2074 -1064y\u00b2 +784)] / (576y\u00b2) -133/4 y\u00b2 +49 +17y\u00b2 =10\n\nSimplify polynomial terms:\n\n-133/4 y\u00b2 +17y\u00b2 = (-133/4 +68/4)y\u00b2 = (-65/4)y\u00b2\n\nSo equation becomes:\n\n[26*(361y\u2074 -1064y\u00b2 +784)] / (576y\u00b2) -65/4 y\u00b2 +49 =10\n\nSubtract 10:\n\n[26*(361y\u2074 -1064y\u00b2 +784)] / (576y\u00b2) -65/4 y\u00b2 +39 =0\n\nThis still looks very complicated. Maybe there's a better approach. Let me think.\n\nAlternatively, maybe instead of eliminating variables by scaling equations, I can express the system as a linear combination. Since both equations are quadratic, perhaps we can find a linear combination that results in a simpler equation, like a homogeneous equation.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps dividing the two equations to eliminate the constant term? Let me see.\n\nIf I divide equation (1) by 10 and equation (2) by 6, then the right-hand sides become 1. So:\n\nEquation (1)/10: (26/10)x\u00b2 - (42/10)xy + (17/10)y\u00b2 =1\n\nEquation (2)/6: (10/6)x\u00b2 - (18/6)xy + (8/6)y\u00b2 =1\n\nSimplify:\n\n(13/5)x\u00b2 - (21/5)xy + (17/10)y\u00b2 =1\n\n(5/3)x\u00b2 - 3xy + (4/3)y\u00b2 =1\n\nNow both equations equal 1. So set them equal to each other:\n\n(13/5)x\u00b2 - (21/5)xy + (17/10)y\u00b2 = (5/3)x\u00b2 -3xy + (4/3)y\u00b2\n\nBring all terms to left-hand side:\n\n(13/5 -5/3)x\u00b2 + (-21/5 +3)xy + (17/10 -4/3)y\u00b2 =0\n\nCompute each coefficient:\n\nFor x\u00b2: 13/5 -5/3 = (39 -25)/15 =14/15\n\nFor xy: -21/5 +15/5 = (-6/5)\n\nFor y\u00b2:17/10 -4/3 = (51 -40)/30 =11/30\n\nSo equation becomes:\n\n(14/15)x\u00b2 - (6/5)xy + (11/30)y\u00b2 =0\n\nMultiply all terms by 30 to eliminate denominators:\n\n28x\u00b2 -36xy +11y\u00b2 =0\n\nSo now we have a homogeneous quadratic equation:28x\u00b2 -36xy +11y\u00b2 =0\n\nThis might be factorable. Let me check.\n\nLooking for factors of the form (ax + by)(cx + dy)=0.\n\nWe have 28x\u00b2 -36xy +11y\u00b2.\n\nFind a and c such that a*c=28, and b*d=11. Also, ad + bc = -36.\n\nPossible factors:\n\n28 can be 4*7, 2*14, 1*28\n\n11 is prime, so b and d must be 1 and 11 or 11 and 1, with signs. Since the middle term is -36xy, which is negative, and the product term is +11y\u00b2, so signs for b and d must be both negative.\n\nSo let's try (4x - y)(7x -11y). Let's check:\n\nFirst term:4x*7x=28x\u00b2\n\nLast term: (-y)(-11y)=11y\u00b2\n\nMiddle term:4x*(-11y) + (-y)*7x= -44xy -7xy= -51xy. Not -36xy. Not matching.\n\nAnother try: (14x -11y)(2x - y)\n\nFirst term:14x*2x=28x\u00b2\n\nLast term: (-11y)(-y)=11y\u00b2\n\nMiddle term:14x*(-y) + (-11y)*2x= -14xy -22xy= -36xy. Perfect!\n\nYes! So:\n\n28x\u00b2 -36xy +11y\u00b2 = (14x -11y)(2x - y)=0\n\nTherefore, either 14x -11y =0 or 2x - y=0\n\nSo we have two cases:\n\nCase 1:14x -11y =0 \u21d2 y = (14/11)x\n\nCase 2:2x -y =0 \u21d2 y=2x\n\nNow, for each case, substitute back into one of the original equations to solve for x and y.\n\nLet's start with Case 1: y = (14/11)x\n\nSubstitute y = (14/11)x into equation (2):10x\u00b2 -18xy +8y\u00b2=6\n\nCompute each term:\n\nx remains x\n\ny=14/11 x\n\nxy =x*(14/11 x)=14/11 x\u00b2\n\ny\u00b2=(14/11 x)^2=196/121 x\u00b2\n\nSubstitute into equation (2):\n\n10x\u00b2 -18*(14/11 x\u00b2) +8*(196/121 x\u00b2)=6\n\nCompute each coefficient:\n\n10x\u00b2 - (18*14)/11 x\u00b2 + (8*196)/121 x\u00b2\n\nCalculate:\n\n18*14=252 \u21d2 252/11\n\n8*196=1568 \u21d21568/121\n\nSo:\n\n10x\u00b2 -252/11 x\u00b2 +1568/121 x\u00b2 =6\n\nConvert all terms to denominator 121:\n\n10x\u00b2 = 1210/121 x\u00b2\n\n-252/11 x\u00b2 = -252*11/121 x\u00b2= -2772/121 x\u00b2\n\n1568/121 x\u00b2 remains the same.\n\nSo total:\n\n(1210 -2772 +1568)/121 x\u00b2 =6\n\nCompute numerator:\n\n1210 -2772 = -1562\n\n-1562 +1568=6\n\nSo:\n\n6/121 x\u00b2 =6\n\nMultiply both sides by 121/6:\n\nx\u00b2=121\n\nThus, x=\u00b111\n\nThen y=(14/11)x \u21d2 y=\u00b114\n\nSo solutions from Case 1: (11,14) and (-11,-14)\n\nNow check Case 2:y=2x\n\nSubstitute y=2x into equation (2):10x\u00b2 -18x*(2x) +8*(2x)^2=6\n\nCompute each term:\n\n10x\u00b2 -36x\u00b2 +32x\u00b2=6\n\nCombine like terms:\n\n(10 -36 +32)x\u00b2=6 \u21d26x\u00b2=6 \u21d2x\u00b2=1 \u21d2x=\u00b11\n\nThen y=2x \u21d2 y=\u00b12\n\nThus, solutions from Case 2: (1,2) and (-1,-2)\n\nNow, we need to verify all solutions in the original equations, because sometimes when we divide or manipulate equations, extraneous solutions can be introduced. Let's check each pair.\n\nFirst, check (11,14):\n\nEquation (1):26*(11)^2 -42*11*14 +17*(14)^2\n\nCompute:\n\n26*121=3146\n\n42*11*14=42*154=6468\n\n17*196=3332\n\nSo 3146 -6468 +3332= (3146+3332) -6468=6478 -6468=10. Correct.\n\nEquation (2):10*(11)^2 -18*11*14 +8*(14)^2\n\n10*121=1210\n\n18*11*14=2772\n\n8*196=1568\n\n1210 -2772 +1568= (1210+1568)-2772=2778-2772=6. Correct.\n\nNext, (-11,-14):\n\nEquation (1):26*(-11)^2 -42*(-11)(-14) +17*(-14)^2\n\n26*121=3146\n\n42*11*14=6468 (since both x and y are negative, product is positive)\n\n17*196=3332\n\n3146 -6468 +3332=10. Correct.\n\nEquation (2):10*(-11)^2 -18*(-11)(-14) +8*(-14)^2\n\n10*121=1210\n\n18*11*14=2772\n\n8*196=1568\n\n1210 -2772 +1568=6. Correct.\n\nNow check (1,2):\n\nEquation (1):26*1 -42*1*2 +17*4=26 -84 +68=10. Correct.\n\nEquation (2):10*1 -18*1*2 +8*4=10 -36 +32=6. Correct.\n\n(-1,-2):\n\nEquation (1):26*(-1)^2 -42*(-1)(-2) +17*(-2)^2=26 -84 +68=10. Correct.\n\nEquation (2):10*(-1)^2 -18*(-1)(-2) +8*(-2)^2=10 -36 +32=6. Correct.\n\nAll solutions check out.\n\nTherefore, the solutions are (11,14), (-11,-14), (1,2), (-1,-2).\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe solutions to the system are \\boxed{(1, 2)}, \\boxed{(-1, -2)}, \\boxed{(11, 14)}, and \\boxed{(-11, -14)}.", "deepseek_solution": "To solve the system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{array}{l}\n26 x^{2}-42 x y+17 y^{2}=10 \\\\\n10 x^{2}-18 x y+8 y^{2}=6\n\\end{array}\\right.\n$$\n\nwe start by dividing both equations by their respective constants to simplify them:\n\n1. Divide the first equation by 10:\n   $$\n   \\frac{26}{10}x^2 - \\frac{42}{10}xy + \\frac{17}{10}y^2 = 1\n   $$\n   Simplifies to:\n   $$\n   \\frac{13}{5}x^2 - \\frac{21}{5}xy + \\frac{17}{10}y^2 = 1\n   $$\n\n2. Divide the second equation by 6:\n   $$\n   \\frac{10}{6}x^2 - \\frac{18}{6}xy + \\frac{8}{6}y^2 = 1\n   $$\n   Simplifies to:\n   $$\n   \\frac{5}{3}x^2 - 3xy + \\frac{4}{3}y^2 = 1\n   $$\n\nSetting these two simplified equations equal to each other:\n$$\n\\frac{13}{5}x^2 - \\frac{21}{5}xy + \\frac{17}{10}y^2 = \\frac{5}{3}x^2 - 3xy + \\frac{4}{3}y^2\n$$\n\nMultiplying through by 30 to clear the denominators:\n$$\n28x^2 - 36xy + 11y^2 = 0\n$$\n\nThis quadratic equation factors as:\n$$\n(14x - 11y)(2x - y) = 0\n$$\n\nThis gives two cases:\n1. \\(14x - 11y = 0 \\Rightarrow y = \\frac{14}{11}x\\)\n2. \\(2x - y = 0 \\Rightarrow y = 2x\\)\n\n**Case 1: \\(y = \\frac{14}{11}x\\)**\n\nSubstitute \\(y = \\frac{14}{11}x\\) into the second original equation:\n$$\n10x^2 - 18x\\left(\\frac{14}{11}x\\right) + 8\\left(\\frac{14}{11}x\\right)^2 = 6\n$$\n\nSimplifying, we find:\n$$\n6x^2 = 6 \\Rightarrow x^2 = 1 \\Rightarrow x = \\pm 11\n$$\nThus, \\(y = \\pm 14\\). This gives the solutions \\((11, 14)\\) and \\((-11, -14)\\).\n\n**Case 2: \\(y = 2x\\)**\n\nSubstitute \\(y = 2x\\) into the second original equation:\n$$\n10x^2 - 18x(2x) + 8(2x)^2 = 6\n$$\n\nSimplifying, we find:\n$$\n6x^2 = 6 \\Rightarrow x^2 = 1 \\Rightarrow x = \\pm 1\n$$\nThus, \\(y = \\pm 2\\). This gives the solutions \\((1, 2)\\) and \\((-1, -2)\\).\n\nVerifying all solutions in the original equations confirms they are correct.\n\n### Final Answer\nThe solutions to the system are \\boxed{(1, 2)}, \\boxed{(-1, -2)}, \\boxed{(11, 14)}, and \\boxed{(-11, -14)}.", "ground_truth_solution": "\nTo solve the given system of equations:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n26x^2 - 42xy + 17y^2 = 10 \\\\\n10x^2 - 18xy + 8y^2 = 6\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\n1. **Sum and Difference Method**: Start by summing and subtracting the two equations.\n\n   Let's denote the equations as follows:\n   \\[\n   E_1: 26x^2 - 42xy + 17y^2 = 10\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   E_2: 10x^2 - 18xy + 8y^2 = 6\n   \\]\n\n   Consider the following combinations:\n   \n   **Subtracting \\(E_2\\) from \\(E_1\\)**:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   (26x^2 - 42xy + 17y^2) - (10x^2 - 18xy + 8y^2) &= 10 - 6 \\\\\n   16x^2 - 24xy + 9y^2 &= 4\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n   This simplifies to:\n   \\[\n   (4x - 3y)^2 = 4\n   \\]\n\n   **Summing \\(E_1\\) and \\(E_2\\)**:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   (26x^2 - 42xy + 17y^2) + (10x^2 - 18xy + 8y^2) &= 10 + 6 \\\\\n   36x^2 - 60xy + 25y^2 &= 16\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n   This simplifies to:\n   \\[\n   (6x - 5y)^2 = 16\n   \\]\n\n   Hence, the original system is equivalent to:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{cases}\n   (6x - 5y)^2 = 16 \\\\\n   (4x - 3y)^2 = 4\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Solve for Variables**: Each of these equations gives two possibilities:\n\n   For \\((6x - 5y)^2 = 16\\):\n   \\[\n   6x - 5y = 4 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad 6x - 5y = -4\n   \\]\n\n   For \\((4x - 3y)^2 = 4\\):\n   \\[\n   4x - 3y = 2 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad 4x - 3y = -2\n   \\]\n\n   Thus, we consider all combinations:\n   \n   \\[\n   \\begin{cases}\n   6x - 5y = 4 \\\\\n   4x - 3y = 2\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{cases}\n   6x - 5y = 4 \\\\\n   4x - 3y = -2\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{cases}\n   6x - 5y = -4 \\\\\n   4x - 3y = 2\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{cases}\n   6x - 5y = -4 \\\\\n   4x - 3y = -2\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Solving Each System**:\n\n   For \\(6x - 5y = 4\\) and \\(4x - 3y = 2\\):\n   \\[\n   \\text{Multiply the second equation by 2:} \\quad 8x - 6y = 4\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\text{Now subtract the first equation from this:} \\quad (8x - 6y) - (6x - 5y) = 4 - 4 \\\\\n   2x - y = 0 \\Rightarrow y = 2x\n   \\]\n   Substitute \\(y = 2x\\) into \\(6x - 5y = 4\\):\n   \\[\n   6x - 5(2x) = 4 \\\\\n   6x - 10x = 4 \\\\\n   -4x = 4 \\Rightarrow x = -1, \\ y = -2\n   \\]\n\n   Repeating similar steps for the other systems:\n   \n   \\[\n   \\begin{cases}\n   6x - 5y = 4 \\\\\n   4x - 3y = -2\n   \\end{cases} \n   \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad (x, y) = (1, 2)\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{cases}\n   6x - 5y = -4 \\\\\n   4x - 3y = 2\n   \\end{cases} \n   \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad (x, y) = (-11, -14)\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{cases}\n   6x - 5y = -4 \\\\\n   4x - 3y = -2\n   \\end{cases} \n   \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad (x, y) = (11, 14)\n   \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\n\nThe four solutions to the given system of equations are:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{(-1, -2), \\ (1, 2), \\ (-11, -14), \\ (11, 14)}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "\nIf \\( x \\in \\left[\\frac{1}{27}, 9\\right] \\), then \\( f(x) = \\log_{3} \\frac{x}{27} \\cdot \\log_{3}(3 x) (\\quad) \\).\n\nA. Minimum value is \\( -\\frac{32}{9} \\), maximum value is -3\n\nB. Minimum value is -4, maximum value is 12\n\nC. Minimum value is \\( -\\frac{32}{9} \\), no maximum value\n\nD. No minimum value, maximum value is 12", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let's see. I need to figure out the minimum and maximum values of the function f(x) = log\u2083(x/27) \u00b7 log\u2083(3x) when x is in the interval [1/27, 9]. The options are given, so I need to work through the problem step by step.\n\nFirst, I remember that logarithmic functions can sometimes be simplified using logarithm properties. Let me try to rewrite both logs to make them easier to handle.\n\nStarting with log\u2083(x/27). Using the logarithm property log(a/b) = log a - log b, so this becomes log\u2083x - log\u208327. Since log\u208327 is log\u2083(3\u00b3) which is 3. So, log\u2083(x/27) = log\u2083x - 3.\n\nSimilarly, log\u2083(3x) can be written as log\u20833 + log\u2083x. Log\u20833 is 1, so this simplifies to 1 + log\u2083x.\n\nTherefore, f(x) can be rewritten as (log\u2083x - 3)(1 + log\u2083x). Let me denote y = log\u2083x. Then the function becomes (y - 3)(y + 1). Let me check that again: (log\u2083x - 3)(log\u2083x + 1). Yes, that seems right.\n\nSo, f(x) = (y - 3)(y + 1) where y = log\u2083x. Now, I need to express y in terms of the interval given for x. Since x is in [1/27, 9], let's find the corresponding y values.\n\nSince 1/27 is 3\u207b\u00b3 and 9 is 3\u00b2, then log\u2083(1/27) = -3 and log\u20839 = 2. So, y = log\u2083x is in the interval [-3, 2]. Therefore, y \u2208 [-3, 2].\n\nNow, f(x) becomes a quadratic function in terms of y: f(y) = (y - 3)(y + 1). Let me expand this to standard quadratic form.\n\nMultiplying out the terms: (y - 3)(y + 1) = y\u00b2 + y - 3y - 3 = y\u00b2 - 2y - 3. So, f(y) = y\u00b2 - 2y - 3. This is a quadratic function opening upwards (since the coefficient of y\u00b2 is positive).\n\nTo find the minimum and maximum values of f(y) over y \u2208 [-3, 2], we can analyze the quadratic function. The vertex of a quadratic function f(y) = ay\u00b2 + by + c is at y = -b/(2a). Here, a = 1, b = -2. So the vertex is at y = -(-2)/(2*1) = 2/2 = 1. The vertex is at y = 1, which is within our interval [-3, 2]. Since the parabola opens upwards, this vertex is the minimum point.\n\nSo, the minimum value of f(y) is at y = 1. Plugging back into the equation: f(1) = (1)^2 - 2*(1) - 3 = 1 - 2 - 3 = -4. So, the minimum value is -4.\n\nNow, for the maximum value, since the interval is from y = -3 to y = 2, and the parabola opens upwards, the maximum will occur at one of the endpoints. Let's compute f(y) at y = -3 and y = 2.\n\nFirst, at y = -3: f(-3) = (-3)^2 - 2*(-3) - 3 = 9 + 6 - 3 = 12.\n\nAt y = 2: f(2) = (2)^2 - 2*(2) - 3 = 4 - 4 - 3 = -3.\n\nComparing these two, the maximum value is 12 at y = -3 and the other endpoint 