 rewritten as:\n\\[ f(x) = (y - 3)(y + 1) \\]\n\nSince \\( x \\) is in the interval \\([ \\frac{1}{27}, 9 ]\\), converting \\( x \\) to \\( y \\) gives:\n- \\( \\frac{1}{27} = 3^{-3} \\) so \\( \\log_{3} \\frac{1}{27} = -3 \\)\n- \\( 9 = 3^{2} \\) so \\( \\log_{3} 9 = 2 \\)\n\nThus, \\( y \\) ranges from \\(-3\\) to \\(2\\). The function \\( f(y) \\) becomes a quadratic function:\n\\[ f(y) = (y - 3)(y + 1) = y^2 - 2y - 3 \\]\n\nTo find the critical points and evaluate the function at the endpoints:\n- The vertex of the quadratic function \\( y^2 - 2y - 3 \\) is at \\( y = \\frac{2}{2} = 1 \\), which is within the interval \\([-3, 2]\\).\n- Evaluating \\( f(y) \\) at the critical point and endpoints:\n  - At \\( y = 1 \\): \\( f(1) = 1^2 - 2 \\cdot 1 - 3 = -4 \\)\n  - At \\( y = -3 \\): \\( f(-3) = (-3)^2 - 2 \\cdot (-3) - 3 = 12 \\)\n  - At \\( y = 2 \\): \\( f(2) = 2^2 - 2 \\cdot 2 - 3 = -3 \\)\n\nThe minimum value is \\(-4\\) at \\( y = 1 \\) (corresponding to \\( x = 3 \\)) and the maximum value is \\( 12 \\) at \\( y = -3 \\) (corresponding to \\( x = \\frac{1}{27} \\)).\n\nThus, the correct answer is \\(\\boxed{B}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "To solve the given problem effectively, let's first clearly translate the expression and each calculation step.\n\nGiven the function:\n\\[ f(x) = \\log_3 \\left( \\frac{x}{27} \\right) \\cdot \\log_3(3x) \\]\n\nFirst, we simplify each component inside the logarithms.\n\n1. Simplify \\(\\log_3 \\left( \\frac{x}{27} \\right)\\):\n   \\[\n   \\log_3 \\left( \\frac{x}{27} \\right) = \\log_3 x - \\log_3 27 = \\log_3 x - 3\n   \\]\n\n2. Simplify \\(\\log_3(3x)\\):\n   \\[\n   \\log_3 (3x) = \\log_3 3 + \\log_3 x = 1 + \\log_3 x\n   \\]\n\nNow we plug these back into the function \\(f(x)\\):\n\n\\[\nf(x) = (\\log_3 x - 3) \\cdot (1 + \\log_3 x)\n\\]\n\nNext, we expand the product:\n\n\\[\nf(x) = (\\log_3 x - 3)(1 + \\log_3 x) = \\log_3 x \\cdot 1 + \\log_3 x \\cdot \\log_3 x - 3 \\cdot 1 - 3 \\cdot \\log_3 x\n\\]\n\\[\n= \\lo