's possible to buy two pies that differ in both. So even if some combinations exist where you can pick two that differ in only one aspect, the claim is that there exists at least one pair that differs in both.\n\nAlternatively, maybe it's necessary that such a pair exists. Let's try to structure this.\n\nAssume that the bakery has at least one of each filling and at least one of each method. But not necessarily all combinations. So, possible scenarios:\n\n1. All apple pies are fried, and all cherry pies are baked. Then the available pies are apple fried and cherry baked. If you buy these two, they differ in both filling and method.\n\n2. Some apple pies are fried and some are baked, and same with cherry. Then you can pick, for example, apple fried and cherry baked, which differ in both.\n\nWait, but maybe the bakery has only, say, apple fried and cherry fried. Then they have both fillings (apple and cherry) and both methods? Wait, no. If they only have fried pies (both apple and cherry), then they don't have baked pies. But the problem states that there are two kinds: fried and baked. So the bakery must have both fried and baked pies. Similarly, they must have both apple and cherry fillings. Therefore, the bakery must have at least one fried pie and at least one baked pie, and at least one apple pie and at least one cherry pie. But they might not have all four combinations. For example, they might have apple fried, cherry baked. Then the two pies available are apple fried and cherry baked. Buying those two gives you both differences. Alternatively, they might have apple fried, apple baked, and cherry fried. Here, you can choose apple baked and cherry fried, which differ in both. Or apple fried and cherry fried differ in filling only, apple fried and apple baked differ in method only, but there exists a pair that differs in both.\n\nWait, perhaps the key is that if there are both fillings and both methods present, then there must be at least two pies that form a \"cr