}{\\sqrt{x^4 + 64}}\n\\]\nTherefore, derivative of P(x):\n\\[\nP'(x) = 1 + \\frac{ \\left( \\frac{2x^3}{\\sqrt{x^4 + 64}} \\right) \\cdot x - (8 + \\sqrt{x^4 + 64}) }{x^2}\n\\]\nSimplify numerator of the fraction:\n\\[\n\\frac{2x^4}{\\sqrt{x^4 + 64}} - 8 - \\sqrt{x^4 + 64}\n\\]\nSo:\n\\[\nP'(x) = 1 + \\frac{ \\frac{2x^4}{\\sqrt{x^4 + 64}} - 8 - \\sqrt{x^4 + 64} }{x^2 }\n\\]\nThis looks quite messy. Maybe there's a better approach. Let's consider using the method of Lagrange multipliers for constrained optimization.\n\nWe need to minimize P = x + y + sqrt(x^2 + y^2) subject to xy = 8.\n\nLet me set up the Lagrangian:\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L} = x + y + \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2} - \\lambda (xy - 8)\n\\]\nCompute partial derivatives with respect to x, y, and set them to zero.\n\nFirst, partial derivative with respect to x:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\partial \\mathcal{L}}{\\partial x} = 1 + \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}} - \\lambda y = 0\n\\]\nSimilarly, partial derivative with respect to y:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\partial \\mathcal{L}}{\\partial y} = 1 + \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}} - \\lambda x = 0\n\\]\nAnd the constraint:\n\\[\nxy = 8\n\\]\nSo we have three equations:\n1. \\(1 + \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}} - \\lambda y = 0\\)\n2. \\(1 + \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}} - \\lambda x = 0\\)\n3. \\(xy = 8\\)\n\nLet me denote \\( \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2} = b \\), since in the triangle, b is the hypotenuse. So equations 1 and 2 become:\n1. \\(1 + \\frac{x}{b} - \\lambda y = 0\\)\n2. \\(1 + \\frac{y}{b} - \\lambda x = 0\\)\n\nLet me subtract equation 2 from equation 1:\n\\[\n\\left(1 + \\frac{x}{b} - \\lambda y \\right) - \\left(1 + \\frac{y}{b} - \\lambda x \\right) = 0\n\\]\nSimplify:\n\\[\n\\frac{x - y}{b} - \\lambda (y - x) = 0\n\\]\nFactor out (x - y):\n\\[\n(x - y) \\left( \\frac{1}{b} + \\lambda \\right ) = 0\n\\]\nTherefore, either x = y or \\( \\frac{1}{b} + \\lambda = 0 \\). Let's analyze both possibilities.\n\nCase 1: x = y. If x = y, then from the constraint xy = 8, we have x^2 = 8 => x = y = 2\u221a2. Then