oordinate is proportional to the direction cosine via the same parameter \\(t\\), leading to the equality of the ratios.", "deepseek_solution": "To convert the vector equation \\(\\vec{r} = t\\vec{e}\\) into the coordinate form \\(\\frac{x}{\\cos \\alpha} = \\frac{y}{\\cos \\beta} = \\frac{z}{\\cos \\gamma}\\), follow these steps:\n\n1. **Express the Unit Vector**: Recognize that the unit vector \\(\\vec{e}\\) has components equal to the direction cosines:  \n   \\[\n   \\vec{e} = \\cos \\alpha \\hat{i} + \\cos \\beta \\hat{j} + \\cos \\gamma \\hat{k}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Parametrize the Position Vector**: Let \\(\\vec{r} = x\\hat{i} + y\\hat{j} + z\\hat{k}\\) represent any point \\(M\\) on the line. Substitute \\(\\vec{e}\\) into the vector equation:  \n   \\[\n   x\\hat{i} + y\\hat{j} + z\\hat{k} = t(\\cos \\alpha \\hat{i} + \\cos \\beta \\hat{j} + \\cos \\gamma \\hat{k})\n   \\]\n\n3. **Equate Components**: Match the components to obtain parametric equations:  \n   \\[\n   x = t \\cos \\alpha, \\quad y = t \\cos \\beta, \\quad z = t \\cos \\gamma\n   \\]\n\n4. **Solve for the Parameter \\(t\\)**: Express \\(t\\) in terms of each coordinate:  \n   \\[\n   t = \\frac{x}{\\cos \\alpha}, \\quad t = \\frac{y}{\\cos \\beta}, \\quad t = \\frac{z}{\\cos \\gamma}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Set Ratios Equal**: Since all expressions equal \\(t\\), equate them to eliminate \\(t\\):  \n   \\[\n   \\frac{x}{\\cos \\alpha} = \\frac{y}{\\cos \\beta} = \\frac{z}{\\cos \\gamma}\n   \\]\n\n**Final Coordinate Form**:  \n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{x}{\\cos \\alpha} = \\frac{y}{\\cos \\beta} = \\frac{z}{\\cos \\gamma}}\n\\]\n\n**Note**: If a direction cosine (e.g., \\(\\cos \\gamma\\)) is zero, the corresponding coordinate (\\(z\\)) is zero for all points on the line. The remaining ratios \\(\\frac{x}{\\cos \\alpha} = \\frac{y}{\\cos \\beta}\\) describe the line in the \\(xy\\)-plane.", "ground_truth_solution": "1. **Introduce Direction Cosines**:  \n   Let $\\cos \\alpha, \\cos \\beta, \\cos \\gamma$ be th