h number is taken, the ratio is 17.\n\nTherefore, the final answer is 17.", "deepseek_solution": "The three-digit number can be represented as \\(ABC\\) with digits \\(A\\), \\(B\\), and \\(C\\). The conditions given are:\n1. Removing the hundreds digit \\(A\\) leaves \\(BC = 8A\\), so \\(10B + C = 8A\\).\n2. Removing the units digit \\(C\\) leaves \\(AB = 8C\\), so \\(10A + B = 8C\\).\n\nSolving these equations:\n- From \\(10B + C = 8A\\), express \\(C = 8A - 10B\\).\n- Substitute \\(C\\) into \\(10A + B = 8C\\):  \n  \\(10A + B = 8(8A - 10B)\\)  \n  Simplify to \\(81B = 54A\\), leading to \\(3B = 2A\\).\n\nPossible digit values (since \\(A, B, C\\) are integers and \\(A, C \\geq 2\\)):\n- \\(B = 2\\): \\(A = 3\\), \\(C = 4\\) (number **324**).\n- \\(B = 4\\): \\(A = 6\\), \\(C = 8\\) (number **648**).\n\nFor both numbers:\n- Removing the middle digit \\(B\\) leaves the two-digit number \\(AC\\) (34 or 68).\n- The ratio of \\(AC\\) to \\(B\\) is \\(34/2 = 17\\) or \\(68/4 = 17\\).\n\n**Answer:**  \nThe ratio is \\(\\boxed{17}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "\nLet's denote the three-digit integer as $\\overline{abc}$, where \\(a \\neq 0\\). Given the conditions, we can set up the following equations for the unknown digits \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(c\\):\n\n1. Removing the hundreds place (i.e., \\(a\\)):\n    \\[\n    10b + c = 8a\n    \\]\n\n2. Removing the units place (i.e., \\(c\\)):\n    \\[\n    10a + b = 8c\n    \\]\n\nWe now solve this system of equations. First, let's express \\(a\\) and \\(c\\) in terms of \\(b\\).\n\nRearranging the first equation:\n\\[\n10b + c = 8a \\implies c = 8a - 10b\n\\]\n\nNext, substituting \\(c\\) in the second equation:\n\\[\n10a + b = 8(8a - 10b)\n\\]\n\\[\n10a + b = 64a - 80b\n\\]\n\\[\n10a + b + 80b = 64a\n\\]\n\\[\n10a + 81b = 64a\n\\]\n\\[\n81b = 54a\n\\]\n\\[\nb = \\frac{54a}{81}\n\\]\n\\[\nb = \\frac{2a}{3}\n\\]\n\nNow substitute \\(b = \\frac{2a}{3}\\) into the equation for \\(c\\):\n\\[\nc = 8a - 10\\left(\\frac{2a}{3}\\r