"To solve the problem, we need to determine the vertex angle of the first two identical cones, given that their vertex angle is half that of the fourth cone, and the third cone has a vertex angle of \\(2 \\arcsin \\frac{1}{4}\\). \n\n1. **Define Variables:**\n   - Let the vertex angle of the first two cones be \\(2\\theta\\), so their half-angle is \\(\\theta\\).\n   - The vertex angle of the fourth cone is \\(4\\theta\\), so its half-angle is \\(2\\theta\\).\n   - The third cone has a vertex angle of \\(2 \\arcsin \\frac{1}{4}\\), so its half-angle is \\(\\phi = \\arcsin \\frac{1}{4}\\).\n\n2. **Tangency Conditions:**\n   - Each smaller cone touches the fourth cone internally. The angle between the axis of a smaller cone and the fourth cone's axis is the difference of their half-angles.\n   - For the identical cones (half-angle \\(\\theta\\)), the angle with the fourth cone's axis is \\(2\\theta - \\theta = \\theta\\).\n   - For the third cone (half-angle \\(\\phi\\)), the angle with the fourth cone's axis is \\(2\\theta - \\phi\\).\n\n3. **Spherical Triangle Considerations:**\n   - The three smaller cones form a spherical triangle with angles between their axes being the sum of their half-angles.\n   - The angle between the axes of the two identical cones is \\(2\\theta\\).\n   - The angles between the axes of each identical cone and the third cone are \\(\\theta + \\phi\\).\n\n4. **Using Trigonometric Identities:**\n   - The key equation derived from the tangency conditions and trigonometric identities is:\n     \\[\n     \\cos(2\\theta) - \\sqrt{15} \\sin(2\\theta) = -2\n     \\]\n   - This equation is solved by recognizing it as a cosine of a sum identity, leading to:\n     \\[\n     \\cos(2\\theta + \\delta) = -\\frac{1}{2}\n     \\]\n     where \\(\\delta = \\arctan(\\sqrt{15})\\).\n\n5. **Solving for \\(\\theta\\):**\n   - Using the relationship \\(\\delta = \\frac{\\pi}{2} - \\phi\\), we find:\n     \\[\n     2\\theta + \\frac{\\pi}{2} - \\phi = \\frac{2\\pi}{3}\n     \\]\n   - Simplifying, we get:\n     \\[\n     2\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{6} + \\phi\n     \\]\n   - Substituting \\(\\phi = \\arcsin \\frac{1}{4}\\), the vertex angle of the first two cones is:\n     \\[\n     2\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{6} + \\arcsin \\frac{1}{4}\n     \\]\n\nThus, the vertex angle of the first two cones is \\(\\boxed{\\dfrac{\\pi}{6} + \\arcsin \\dfrac{1}{4}}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. Let \\( 2\\alpha \\) be the required angle at the apex of the first two cones, and let \\( \\beta = \\arcsin \\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right) \\) be the angle at the apex of the third cone.\n\n2. We inscribe spheres with centers \\( O_1, O_2, \\) and \\( O_3 \\) in the first three cones, respectively, such that these spheres touch each other externally, as well as a fourth cone with apex at \\( A \\).\n\n3. Let the fourth cone touch the spheres with centers \\( O_1, O_2, \\) and \\( O_3 \\) at points \\( B, C, \\) and \\( D \\) respectively.\n\n   Observe that distances \\( AB = AC = AD \\), thus these points must lie on a sphere centered at some point \\( O \\), which is inscribed in the fourth cone.\n\n4. Since the fourth cone's axis \\( AO \\) is the axis of symmetry, the angle between \\( AO \\) and any generatrix (slant height) of the fourth cone is also the apex angle of any of the first two identical cones.\n\n5. Consider point \\( H \\) (the tangency point of spheres with centers \\( O_1 \\) and \\( O_2 \\)) as belonging to the plane containing a generatrix \\( AD \\).\n\n6. Now, projecting \\( O_1 \\) and perpendicular \\( O_1 H \\) to the plane of \\( AD \\) gives a relationship involving both \\( \\alpha \\) and \\( \\beta \\). Use the trigonometric identity:\n\n   \\[\n   AO \\cdot \\cos(2 \\alpha - \\beta) = AH \\cdot \\cos(\\alpha + \\beta)\n   \\]\n\n7. This equality simplifies using trigonometric angle addition formulas:\n\n   \\[\n   AH \\cdot \\cos (2\\alpha-\\beta) = \\frac{AB}{\\cos\\alpha} \\cdot \\cos(\\alpha+\\beta)\n   \\]\n\n8. Note \\( AH = AB \\) because \\( A \\)'s tangents to \\( O \\) are equal.\n\n9. Therefore,\n\n   \\[\n   \\cos 2\\alpha \\cos \\beta + \\sin 2\\alpha \\sin \\beta = \\cos \\beta - \\tan \\alpha \\sin \\beta\n   \\]\n\n   Simplify the equation using appropriate trigonometric identities:\n\n   \\[\n   \\cos \\beta (1 - \\cos 2\\alpha) = \\sin \\beta (\\tan \\alpha + \\sin 2\\alpha)\n   \\]\n\n10. Using the trigonometric transformation:\n\n    \\[\n    \\tan \\alpha + \\sin 2\\alpha = \\frac{\\sin 2\\alpha}{1 + \\cos 2\\alpha} + \\sin 2\\alpha = \\sin 2\\alpha \\cdot \\frac{2 + \\cos 2\\alpha}{1 + \\cos 2\\alpha}\n    \\]\n\n    Combine and simplify:\n\n    \\[\n    \\cos \\beta (1 - \\cos^2 2\\alpha) = \\sin \\beta \\sin 2\\alpha (2 + \\cos 2\\alpha)\n    \\]\n\n    Further simplifying yields:\n\n    \\[\n    \\sin (2\\alpha - \\beta) = 2 \\sin \\beta \\Rightarrow 2\\alpha = \\arcsin (2 \\sin \\beta) + \\beta = \\frac{\\pi}{6} + \\arcsin \\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\n    \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\n\nThus, the required angle for the apex of the first two cones is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{\\pi}{6} + \\arcsin \\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "On a board, the numbers $1, 2, \\ldots, 100$ are written. At each stage, all numbers that do not have any divisors among the remaining numbers other than themselves are simultaneously erased. For example, at the first stage, only the number 1 is erased. Which numbers will be erased in the last stage?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let's try to figure out which numbers will be erased in the last stage. The problem says that on a board, numbers from 1 to 100 are written. In each stage, all numbers that do not have any divisors among the remaining numbers other than themselves are erased simultaneously. The example says that in the first stage, only the number 1 is erased. So we need to determine which numbers will be the last ones to be erase