given the trapezoid's configuration, \u03b8 should be in a position such that BC is parallel to AD. Let's consider \u03b8 = 4\u03b1.\n\nBut if \u03b8 =4\u03b1, then point B would be at angle 4\u03b1 from the positive x-axis. Let\u2019s check if this satisfies the slope condition.\n\nIf \u03b8 =4\u03b1, then coordinates of B are (R cos 4\u03b1, R sin 4\u03b1)\n\nThen, the slope of BC is [ -R sin 2\u03b1 - R sin 4\u03b1 ] / [ R cos 2\u03b1 - R cos 4\u03b1 ] = [ -sin 2\u03b1 - sin 4\u03b1 ] / [ cos 2\u03b1 - cos 4\u03b1 ]\n\nSimplify using trigonometric identities:\n\nNumerator: - (sin 2\u03b1 + sin 4\u03b1) = -2 sin 3\u03b1 cos \u03b1\n\nDenominator: cos 2\u03b1 - cos 4\u03b1 = 2 sin 3\u03b1 sin \u03b1\n\nTherefore, slope = [ -2 sin 3\u03b1 cos \u03b1 ] / [ 2 sin 3\u03b1 sin \u03b1 ] = - cot \u03b1\n\nSlope of AD: sin 2\u03b1 / (cos 2\u03b1 -1 ) = [ 2 sin \u03b1 cos \u03b1 ] / [ -2 sin\u00b2 \u03b1 ] = - cot \u03b1\n\nTherefore, the slope condition is satisfied. Therefore, \u03b8 =4\u03b1 is a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, point B is at (R cos 4\u03b1, R sin 4\u03b1)\n\nThus, we have the coordinates of all points:\n\nA: (R,0)\n\nD: (R cos 2\u03b1, R sin 2\u03b1)\n\nC: (R cos 2\u03b1, -R sin 2\u03b1)\n\nB: (R cos 4\u03b1, R sin 4\u03b1)\n\nNow, trapezoid ABCD with bases AD and BC, and legs AB and CD.\n\nNow, the other trapezoid ACDE has bases AC and DE. Since AC is a base of ACDE, which is an isosceles trapezoid, so DE is parallel to AC, and legs CD and AE are equal.\n\nPoint E must be positioned such that DE is parallel to AC, and AE = CD.\n\nCD is the same as in trapezoid ABCD, which is 2R sin 2\u03b1 (distance between C and D).\n\nAE must be equal to CD, so AE = 2R sin 2\u03b1. Since A is at (R,0), point E must be located such that the distance from A to E is 2R sin 2\u03b1.\n\nBut point E is also on the circle, so its coordinates are (R cos \u03c6, R sin \u03c6) for some angle \u03c6.\n\nTherefore, the distance AE is 2R sin(\u0394/2), where \u0394 is the central angle between A and E. Therefore, 2R sin(\u0394/2) = 2R sin 2\u03b1 => sin(\u0394/2) = sin 2\u03b1 => \u0394/2 = 2\u03b1 or \u03c0 -2\u03b1 => \u0394 =4\u03b1 or 2\u03c0 -4\u03b1\n\nBut DE must be parallel to AC. The line DE is between points D(R cos 2\u03b1, R sin 2\u03b1) and E(R cos \u03c6, R sin \u03c6), and it should be parallel to AC, which is between A(R,0) and C(R cos 2\u03b1, -R sin 2\u03b1). The slope of AC is [ -R sin 2\u03b1 -0 ] / [ R cos 2\u03b1 - R ] = -sin 2\u03b1 / (cos 2\u03b1 -1 )\n\nSimilarly, the slope of DE must be the same.\n\nCoordinates of D: (R cos 2\u03b1, R sin 2\u03b1)\n\nCoordinates of E: (R cos \u03c6, R sin \u03c6)\n\nSlope of DE: [ R sin \u03c6 - R sin 2\u03b1 ] / [ R cos \u03c6 - R cos 2\u03b1 ] = [ sin \u03c6 - sin 2\u03b1 ] / [ cos \u03c6 - cos 2\u03b1 ]\n\nThis must equal the slope of AC, which is -sin 2\u03b1 / (cos 2\u03b1 -1 )\n\nTherefore:\n\n[ sin \u03c6 - sin 2\u03b1 ] / [ cos \u03c6 - cos 2\u03b1 ] = -sin 2\u03b1 / (cos 2\u03b1 -1 )\n\nMultiply numerator and denominator on the right by -1:\n\n[ sin \u03c6 - sin 2\u03b1 ] / [ cos \u03c6 - cos 2\u03b1 ] = sin 2\u03b1 / (1 - cos 2\u03b1 )\n\nUsing the identity 1 - cos 2\u03b1 = 2 sin\u00b2 \u03b1, and sin 2\u03b1 = 2 sin \u03b1 cos \u03b1:\n\nRight side: (2 sin \u03b1 cos \u03b1) / (2 sin\u00b2 \u03b1) = cot \u03b1\n\nLeft side: [ sin \u03c6 - sin 2\u03b1 ] / [ cos \u03c6 - cos 2\u03b1 ]\n\nUsing the identities:\n\nsin \u03c6 - sin 2\u03b1 = 2 cos((\u03c6 + 2\u03b1)/2) sin((\u03c6 - 2\u03b1)/2)\n\ncos \u03c6 - cos 2\u03b1 = -2 sin((\u03c6 + 2\u03b1)/2) sin((\u03c6 - 2\u03b1)/2)\n\nThus, the left side becomes:\n\n[ 2 cos((\u03c6 + 2\u03b1)/2) sin((\u03c6 - 2\u03b1)/2) ] / [ -2 sin((\u03c6 + 2\u03b1)/2) sin((\u03c6 - 2\u03b1)/2) ) ] = -cot((\u03c6 + 2\u03b1)/2)\n\nTherefore:\n\n-cot((\u03c6 + 2\u03b1)/2) = cot \u03b1\n\n=> cot((\u03c6 + 2\u03b1)/2) = -cot \u03b1 = cot(\u03c0 - \u03b1)\n\nTherefore:\n\n(\u03c6 + 2\u03b1)/2 = \u03c0 - \u03b1 + k\u03c0\n\nMultiply both sides by 2:\n\n\u03c6 + 2\u03b1 = 2\u03c0 - 2\u03b1 + 2k\u03c0\n\nThus:\n\n\u03c6 = 2\u03c0 -4\u03b1 + 2k\u03c0\n\nSince \u03c6 is an angle in the circle, we can take k=0:\n\n\u03c6 = 2\u03c0 -4\u03b1\n\nTherefore, point E is at angle 2\u03c0 -4\u03b1, which is equivalent to angle -4\u03b1. So, coordinates of E are (R cos(-4\u03b1), R sin(-4\u03b1)) = (R cos 4\u03b1, -R sin 4\u03b1)\n\nTherefore, point E is at (R cos 4\u03b1, -R sin 4\u03b1)\n\nNow, we have all points defined in terms of \u03b1 and R:\n\nA: (R,0)\n\nB: (R cos 4\u03b1, R sin 4\u03b1)\n\nC: (R cos 2\u03b1, -R sin 2\u03b1)\n\nD: (R cos 2\u03b1, R sin 2\u03b1)\n\nE: (R cos 4\u03b1, -R sin 4\u03b1)\n\nNow, we need to find the area of triangle ADE, which is given as 1 + \u221a3.\n\nLet\u2019s compute the coordinates of points A, D, and E:\n\nA: (R,0)\n\nD: (R cos 2\u03b1, R sin 2\u03b1)\n\nE: (R cos 4\u03b1, -R sin 4\u03b1)\n\nWe can use the shoelace formula to compute the area of triangle ADE.\n\nCoordinates:\n\nA: (R,0)\n\nD: (R cos 2\u03b1, R sin 2\u03b1)\n\nE: (R cos 4\u03b1, -R sin 4\u03b1)\n\nThe area is given by:\n\n1/2 | (x_A(y_D - y_E) + x_D(y_E - y_A) + x_E(y_A - y_D)) |\n\nPlugging in:\n\n=1/2 | R(R sin 2\u03b1 - (-R sin 4\u03b1)) + R cos 2\u03b1(-R sin 4\u03b1 -0) + R cos 4\u03b1(0 - R sin 2\u03b1) |\n\nSimplify:\n\n=1/2 | R(R sin 2\u03b1 + R sin 4\u03b1) + R cos 2\u03b1(-R sin 4\u03b1) + R cos 4\u03b1(-R sin 2\u03b1) |\n\n=1/2 | R^2 sin 2\u03b1 + R^2 sin 4\u03b1 - R^2 cos 2\u03b1 sin 4\u03b1 - R^2 cos 4\u03b1 sin 2\u03b1 |\n\nFactor out R^2:\n\n= (R^2)/2 | sin 2\u03b1 + sin 4\u03b1 - cos 2\u03b1 sin 4\u03b1 - cos 4\u03b1 sin 2\u03b1 |\n\nSimplify the terms inside:\n\nLet\u2019s look at the terms sin 4\u03b1 - cos 2\u03b1 sin 4\u03b1 - cos 4\u03b1 sin 2\u03b1 + sin 2\u03b1\n\nFirst, note that sin 4\u03b1 = 2 sin 2\u03b1 cos 2\u03b1\n\nSo, substituting:\n\n= sin 2\u03b1 + 2 sin 2\u03b1 cos 2\u03b1 - cos 2\u03b1*(2 sin 2\u03b1 cos 2\u03b1) - cos 4\u03b1 sin 2\u03b1\n\nSimplify term by term:\n\n1. sin 2\u03b1