pha -1) = Kt + 2K cos 2alpha\n\nBring all terms involving t to the left:\n\nt(cos 2alpha -1 - K ) = 2K cos 2alpha - (1 - cos 4alpha )\n\nLet\u2019s compute K:\n\nK = (cos 2alpha - cos 4alpha ) * cos alpha / cos 3alpha\n\nUsing identities:\n\ncos 4alpha = 2 cos^2 2alpha -1\n\ncos 2alpha - cos 4alpha = cos 2alpha - 2 cos^2 2alpha +1 = -2 cos^2 2alpha + cos 2alpha +1\n\nBut not sure. Alternatively, express in terms of sin:\n\ncos 2alpha - cos 4alpha = 2 sin 3alpha sin alpha\n\nTherefore, K = 2 sin 3alpha sin alpha * cos alpha / cos 3alpha\n\n= 2 sin alpha cos alpha * sin 3alpha / cos 3alpha\n\n= sin 2alpha * tan 3alpha\n\nTherefore, K = sin 2alpha * tan 3alpha\n\nNow, substitute K back into the equation:\n\nt(cos 2alpha -1 - sin 2alpha tan 3alpha ) = 2 sin 2alpha tan 3alpha cos 2alpha - (1 - cos 4alpha )\n\nThis is getting extremely complicated. Maybe there's a better approach using the properties of the trapezoid and the circle.\n\nGiven that angle COD is 60\u00b0, and we have expressions for OC, OD, and CD in terms of R and alpha.\n\nFrom earlier, we have:\n\nIn triangle COD:\n\nOC = (2R sin alpha sin 3alpha )/(sin alpha + sin 3alpha )\n\nOD = R sin alpha\n\nCD = 2R sin 2alpha\n\nAngle COD =60\u00b0, so applying the Law of Cosines:\n\nCD\u00b2 = OC\u00b2 + OD\u00b2 - 2 OC * OD cos 60\u00b0\n\nPlugging in:\n\n(2R sin 2alpha )\u00b2 = [ (2R sin alpha sin 3alpha )/(sin alpha + sin 3alpha ) ]\u00b2 + (R sin alpha )\u00b2 - 2 * [ (2R sin alpha sin 3alpha )/(sin alpha + sin 3alpha ) ] * R sin alpha * (1/2)\n\nSimplify:\n\n4R\u00b2 sin\u00b2 2alpha = [ 4R\u00b2 sin\u00b2 alpha sin\u00b2 3alpha ] / (sin alpha + sin 3alpha )\u00b2 + R\u00b2 sin\u00b2 alpha - [ 2R\u00b2 sin alpha sin 3alpha * R sin alpha ] / (sin alpha + sin 3alpha )\n\nWait, let's carefully compute each term:\n\nLeft side: 4R\u00b2 sin\u00b2 2alpha\n\nFirst term on right: [2R sin alpha sin 3alpha / (sin alpha + sin 3alpha )]^2 = 4R\u00b2 sin\u00b2 alpha sin\u00b2 3alpha / (sin alpha + sin 3alpha )\u00b2\n\nSe