u0445 \u0442\u043e\u0447\u0435\u043a \u043f\u0435\u0440\u0435\u0441\u0435\u0447\u0435\u043d\u0438\u044f:**\n   \u0420\u0430\u0441\u0441\u043c\u043e\u0442\u0440\u0438\u043c \u0442\u0430\u043a\u0436\u0435 \u0442\u043e\u0447\u043a\u0438 \\( A_b \\) \u0438 \\( A_c \\), \u0430 \u0442\u0430\u043a\u0436\u0435 \u0442\u043e\u0447\u043a\u0438 \\( B_a \\) \u0438 \\( B_c \\) \u043f\u0435\u0440\u0435\u0441\u0435\u0447\u0435\u043d\u0438\u044f \u0434\u0440\u0443\u0433\u0438\u0445 \u0442\u0440\u0435\u0445 \u0431\u043e\u043b\u044c\u0448\u0438\u0445 \u043a\u0440\u0443\u0433\u043e\u0432:\n   \n   - \u0422\u043e\u0447\u043a\u0430 \\( A_b \\) \u043f\u0440\u0438\u043d\u0430\u0434\u043b\u0435\u0436\u0438\u0442 \u0434\u0443\u0433\u0435 \\( b \\).\n   - \u0422\u043e\u0447\u043a\u0430 \\( A_c \\) \u043f\u0440\u0438\u043d\u0430\u0434\u043b\u0435\u0436\u0438\u0442 \u0434\u0443\u0433\u0435 \\( c \\).\n   - \u0422\u043e\u0447\u043a\u0430 \\( B_a \\) \u043f\u0440\u0438\u043d\u0430\u0434\u043b\u0435\u0436\u0438\u0442 \u0434\u0443\u0433\u0435 \\( a \\).\n   - \u0422\u043e\u0447\u043a\u0430 \\( B_c \\) \u043f\u0440\u0438\u043d\u0430\u0434\u043b\u0435\u0436\u0438\u0442 \u0434\u0443\u0433\u0435 \\( c \\).\n\n4. **\u0420\u0430\u0441\u0441\u043c\u043e\u0442\u0440\u0435\u043d\u0438\u0435 \u0443\u0433\u043b\u043e\u0432 \u043d\u0430 \u0441\u0444\u0435\u0440\u0435:**\n   - \u0422\u043e\u0447\u043a\u0438 \\( B_c \\) \u0438 \\( C_b \\) \u043b\u0435\u0436\u0430\u0442 \u0432 \u043f\u043b\u043e\u0441\u043a\u043e\u0441\u0442\u0438 \u0434\u0443\u0433\u0438 \\( a \\), \u043d\u043e \u0441\u0430\u043c\u0438 \u0434\u0443\u0433\u0430 \\( a \\)  \u0438 \u043f\u0440\u0438\u043d\u0430\u0434\u043b\u0435\u0436\u0430\u0442. \u0421\u043b\u0435\u0434\u043e\u0432\u0430\u0442\u0435\u043b\u044c\u043d\u043e, \u0443\u0433\u043e\u043b \\(\\angle B_c O C_b \\) (\u0437\u0434\u0435\u0441\u044c \\( O \\) \u2014 \u0446\u0435\u043d\u0442\u0440 \u0441\u0444\u0435\u0440\u044b) \u0431\u0443\u0434\u0435\u0442 \u043c\u0435\u043d\u044c\u0448\u0435 \\(60^{\\circ}\\) (\u0442\u0430\u043a \u043a\u0430\u043a \u043a\u0430\u0436\u0434\u0430\u044f \u0434\u0443\u0433\u0430 \u043f\u0440\u043e\u0441\u0442\u0438\u0440\u0430\u0435\u0442\u0441\u044f \u043d\u0430 \\(300^{\\circ}\\), \u0434\u0435\u043b\u0430\u0435\u0442 \u0443\u0433\u043b\u044b \u043c\u0435\u043d\u0435\u0435 \\(180^{\\circ} - 2 \\times 60^{\\circ} = 60^{\\circ}\\))\n\n5. **\u0420\u0430\u0441\u0441\u043c\u043e\u0442\u0440\u0435\u043d\u0438\u0435 \u0434\u0440\u0443\u0433\u0438\u0445 \u0443\u0433\u043b\u043e\u0432:**\n   \u0410\u043d\u0430\u043b\u043e\u0433\u0438\u0447\u043d\u043e, \\(\\angle A_c O C_a < 60^{\\circ}\\) \u0438 \\(\\angle A_b O B_a < 60^{\\circ} \\).\n\n6. **\u041f\u0440\u0438\u043c\u0435\u043d\u0435\u043d\u0438\u0435 \u0441\u0443\u043c\u043c\u044b \u0443\u0433\u043b\u043e\u0432:**\n   - \u0421\u043b\u0435\u0434\u043e\u0432\u0430\u0442\u0435\u043b\u044c\u043d\u043e, \\(\\angle A_c O B_c < 60^{\\circ}\\)\n   - \u0422\u0430\u043a\u0436\u0435, \\(\\angle A_b O B_a < 60^{\\circ}\\)\n   - \u0422\u043e\u0433\u0434\u0430 \\(\\angle A_c O B_c + \\angle B_c O C_b < \\angle A_c O C_b\\)\n\n7. **\u041f\u0440\u043e\u0442\u0438\u0432\u043e\u0440\u0435\u0447\u0438\u0435:**\n   \u0421\u043b\u0435\u0434\u043e\u0432\u0430\u0442\u0435\u043b\u044c\u043d\u043e, \\(\\angle A_c O B_c + \\angle B_c O C_b < \\angle A_c O C_b\\), \u0447\u0442\u043e \u044f\u0432\u043b\u044f\u0435\u0442\u0441\u044f \u043f\u0440\u043e\u0442\u0438\u0432\u043e\u0440\u0435\u0447\u0438\u0435\u043c, \u0442\u0430\u043a \u043a\u0430\u043a \u043f\u0440\u0438 \u043d\u0435\u043f\u0440\u0430\u0432\u0438\u043b\u044c\u043d\u043e\u043c \u043f\u0435\u0440\u0435\u0441\u0435\u0447\u0435\u043d\u0438\u0438 \u0438\u0445 \u0441\u0443\u043c\u043c\u044b \u0434\u043e\u043b\u0436\u043d\u044b \u0431\u044b\u0442\u044c \u0440\u0430\u0432\u043d\u044b \\(180^{\\circ}\\).\n\n### Conclusion:\n\u0422\u0430\u043a\u0438\u043c \u043e\u0431\u0440\u0430\u0437\u043e\u043c, \u043d\u0430\u0448\u0435 \u043f\u0440\u0435\u0434\u043f\u043e\u043b\u043e\u0436\u0435\u043d\u0438\u0435, \u0447\u0442\u043e \u0434\u0443\u0433\u0438 \\( a \\), \\( b \\), \u0438 \\( c \\) \u043d\u0435 \u043f\u0435\u0440\u0435\u0441\u0435\u043a\u0430\u044e\u0442\u0441\u044f, \u043e\u043a\u0430\u0437\u0430\u043b\u043e\u0441\u044c \u043d\u0435\u0432\u0435\u0440\u043d\u044b\u043c. \u041f\u043e\u044d\u0442\u043e\u043c\u0443, \u043a\u0430\u043a \u043c\u0438\u043d\u0438\u043c\u0443\u043c \u0434\u0432\u0435 \u0438\u0437 \u044d\u0442\u0438\u0445 \u0434\u0443\u0433 \u043e\u0431\u044f\u0437\u0430\u0442\u0435\u043b\u044c\u043d\u043e \u043f\u0435\u0440\u0435\u0441\u0435\u043a\u0430\u044e\u0442\u0441\u044f.\n\\[\\boxed{\\text{\u0422\u0435\u043e\u0440\u0435\u043c\u0430 \u0434\u043e\u043a\u0430\u0437\u0430\u043d\u0430}}\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "A circle touches one of the legs of an isosceles right triangle and passes through the vertex of the opposite acute angle. Find the radius of the circle if its center lies on the hypotenuse of the triangle, and the leg of the triangle is equal to \\(a\\).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let me try to solve this geometry problem. So, we have an isosceles right triangle with legs of length \\(a\\). A circle touches one of the legs, passes through the opposite acute angle's vertex, and its center is on the hypotenuse. We need to find the radius of this circle. Hmm, let me visualize the problem first.\n\nFirst, let's sketch the triangle. An isosceles right triangle has two legs of equal length \\(a\\) and the hypotenuse. Let's name the triangle ABC, where angle C is the right angle, and legs AC and BC are each of length \\(a\\). The hypotenuse is AB. The acute angles are at A and B, each 45 degrees. The circle touches one leg, say AC, and passes through vertex B (the opposite acute angle). The center of the circle lies on hypotenuse AB. So, the circle is tangent to leg AC, passes through point B, and its center is somewhere on AB.\n\nLet me denote the center of the circle as O. Since the center lies on AB, we can parameterize its position along the hypotenuse. Let's set up coordinates to model this scenario. Maybe coordinate geometry will help here.\n\nLet\u2019s place point C at the origin (0,0), so AC is along the x-axis from (0,0) to (a,0), and BC is along the y-axis from (0,0) to (0,a). Then, hypotenuse AB connects (a,0) to (0,a). The equation of hypotenuse AB can be found. Since it goes from (a,0) to (0,a), its slope is -1, and the equation is \\(y = -x + a\\).\n\nThe center O is somewhere on AB, so its coordinates can be represented as (h, k), where \\(k = -h + a\\). Therefore, O is (h, -h + a).\n\nSince the circle is tangent to leg AC, which is the x-axis (y=0). The distance from the center O to the x-axis must be equal to the radius r of the circle. The distance from a point (h, k) to the x-axis is |k|. Since our circle is above the x-axis (because it passes through B which is at (0,a)), k must be positive. Therefore, the radius r = k = -h + a. So, r = -h + a. Hence, h = a - r.\n\nAlso, the circle passes through point B, which is at (0,a). The distance from the center O (h, -h + a) to point B (0,a) must be equal to the radius r. Let's write that equation:\n\nDistance OB = r.\n\nUsing the distance formula: sqrt[(h - 0)^2 + (-h + a - a)^2] = r\n\nSimplify inside the square root:\n\nsqrt[h^2 + (-h)^2] = sqrt[h^2 + h^2] = sqrt[2h^2] = h*sqrt(2)\n\nSo, h*sqrt(2) = r\n\nBut earlier, we had r = -h + a. Therefore:\n\nh*sqrt(2) = -h + a\n\nLet me write this equation:\n\nh*sqrt(2) + h = a\n\nFactor h:\n\nh (sqrt(2) + 1) = a\n\nTherefore:\n\nh = a / (sqrt(2) + 1)\n\nBut h = a - r, so:\n\na - r = a / (sqrt(2) + 1)\n\nSolving for r:\n\nr = a - [a / (sqrt(2) + 1)] = a [1 - 1/(sqrt(2) + 1)]\n\nLet me rationalize the denominator in 1/(sqrt(2) + 1):\n\nMultiply numerator and denominator by (sqrt(2) - 1):\n\n1/(sqrt(2) + 1) = (sqrt(2) - 1)/[(sqrt(2) + 1)(sqrt(2) - 1)] = (sqrt(2) - 1)/(2 - 1) = sqrt(2) - 1\n\nTherefore:\n\nr = a [1 - (sqrt(2) - 1)] = a [1 - sqrt(2) + 1] = a [2 - sqrt(2)]\n\nSo, the radius is \\(a(2 - \\sqrt{2})\\).\n\nWait, let me verify this result. Let's check if this makes sense. If we plug back h = a - r into the equation h*sqrt(2) = r, substituting r = a(2 - sqrt(2)):\n\nh = a - a(2 - sqrt(2)) = a[1 - 2 + sqrt(2)] = a(-1 + sqrt(2))\n\nThen, h*sqrt(2) = a(-1 + sqrt(2)) * sqrt(2) = a(-sqrt(2) + 2). Which is equal to a(2 - sqrt(2)), which is r. So, that checks out.\n\nAlternatively, let's compute h as a/(sqrt(2) + 1). Multiply numerator and denominator by (sqrt(2) - 1):\n\nh = a (sqrt(2) - 1)/[(sqrt(2) + 1)(sqrt(2) - 1)] = a (sqrt(2) -1)/1 = a (sqrt(2) -1)\n\nThen, r = h*sqrt(2) = a (sqrt(2) -1) sqrt(2) = a (2 - sqrt(2)). Yes, same result.\n\nTherefore, the radius is \\(a(2 - \\sqrt{2})\\).\n\nWait, but let's make sure the circle is indeed tangent to leg AC. The distance from center O to AC (the x-axis) is equal to the radius. Since O is at (h, k) where k = -h + a, so the y-coordinate is k = -h + a. Since h = a/(sqrt(2) +1), then k = -a/(sqrt(2)+1) + a = a [1 - 1/(sqrt(2)+1)] = same as r, which is correct. So, the radius is equal to the y-coordinate of the center, which is the distance to the x-axis (leg AC), so tangency is satisfied.\n\nAlso, the circle passes through point B (0,a). Let's check the distance from O (h, k) to B (0,a):\n\nDistance squared is (h - 0)^2 + (k - a)^2 = h^2 + (-h + a - a)^2 = h^2 + (-h)^2 = 2h^2. Which should equal r^2.\n\nGiven that r = a(2 - sqrt(2)), let's compute r^2:\n\nr^2 = a^2 (4 - 4 sqrt(2) + 2) = a^2 (6 - 4 sqrt(2))\n\nOn the other hand, 2h^2 where h = a (sqrt(2)-1):\n\n2 [a^2 (sqrt(2)-1)^2] = 2 a^2 [ (2 - 2 sqrt(2) +1 ) ] = 2 a^2 (3 - 2 sqrt(2)) = a^2 (6 -4 sqrt(2))\n\nSo, they are equal. Therefore, this checks out.\n\nTherefore, after going through coordinate geometry, setting up equations, solving them, and verifying the results, the radius is indeed \\(a(2 - \\sqrt{2})\\).\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe radius of the circle is \\boxed{a(2 - \\sqrt{2})}.", "deepseek_solution": "Given an isosceles right triangle with legs of length \\(a\\), we need to find the radius of a circle that touches one of the legs, passes through the vertex of the opposite acute angle, and has its center on the hypotenuse.\n\n1. **Coordinate Setup**:\n   - Place the right angle vertex \\(C\\) at the origin \\((0,0)\\).\n   - Legs \\(AC\\) and \\(BC\\) are along the x-axis and y-axis, respectively, with coordinates \\(A(a,0)\\) and \\(B(0,a)\\).\n   - The hypotenuse \\(AB\\) has the equation \\(y = -x + a\\).\n\n2. **Center of the Circle**:\n   - The center \\(O\\) lies on the hypotenuse \\(AB\\), so its coordinates are \\((h, -h + a)\\).\n   - The radius \\(r\\) of the circle is the distance from \\(O\\) to the x-axis (leg \\(AC\\)), which is \\(r = -h + a\\).\n\n3. **Distance from Center to Vertex \\(B\\)**:\n   - The circle passes through vertex \\(B(0,a)\\), so the distance from \\(O(h, -h + a)\\) to \\(B(0,a)\\) must be equal to \\(r\\).\n   - Using the distance formula: \n     \\[\n     \\sqrt{(h - 0)^2 + (-h + a - a)^2} = r\n     \\]\n   - Simplifying, we get:\n     \\[\n     \\sqrt{h^2 + (-h)^2} = \\sqrt{2h^2} = h\\sqrt{2} = r\n     \\]\n\n4. **Solving for \\(h\\)**:\n   - Equating the two expressions for \\(r\\):\n     \\[\n     h\\sqrt{2} = -h + a\n     \\]\n   - Solving for \\(h\\):\n     \\[\n     h(\\sqrt{2} + 1) = a \\implies h = \\frac{a}{\\sqrt{2} + 1}\n     \\]\n   - Rationalizing the denominator:\n     \\[\n     h = \\frac{a(\\sqrt{2} - 1)}{(\\sqrt{2} + 1)(\\sqrt{2} - 1)} = a(\\sqrt{2} - 1)\n     \\]\n\n5. **Finding the Radius \\(r\\)**:\n   - Substituting \\(h = a(\\sqrt{2} - 1)\\) into \\(r = -h + a\\):\n     \\[\n     r = -a(\\sqrt{2} - 1) + a = a - a(\\sqrt{2} - 1) = a(2 - \\sqrt{2})\n     \\]\n\nThus, the radius of the circle is \\(\\boxed{a(2 - \\sqrt{2})}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Consider the setup:**\n\n   Note that there is an isosceles right-angled triangle with legs of length $a$. Let's position the right-angled triangle in the coordinate plane such that its hypotenuse lies along the line $AB$, where $AB$ is the hypotenuse. The given circle has its center $O$ on the hypotenuse $AB$, touches the leg $BC$ at point $K$, and passes through vertex $A$.\n\n2. **Parameters and notation:**\n\n   Denote the radius of the circle as $x$. Denote the coordinates of the vertices as:\n   - $A(0, 0)$\n   - $B(a, 0)$\n   - $C(0, a)$\n\n3. **Properties of the circle:**\n\n   The circle touches the leg $BC$ at point $K$, which has a distance $x$ from the point $O$ to the line $BC$. The circle passes through the vertex $A$.\n\n4. **Distance from the center to the vertices:**\n\n   The distance between the center $O$ of the circle on the hypotenuse $AB$ to vertex $A$ is the radius.\n   \\[\n   OA = x \\implies O(0, x)\n   \\]\n\n5. **Construct a right triangle involving radius:**\n\n   Consider the right triangle $OBK$. Since $\\angle B$ is $45^\\circ$ in isosceles right triangles,\n   \\[\n   OK = x\n   \\]\n\n6. **Calculating the distance OB:**\n\n   The legs are equal, so use the Pythagorean theorem for the $45^\\circ$-angle triangle,\n   \\[\n   OB = a\\sqrt{2} - x\n   \\]\n\n7. **Setting up the radius equalities:**\n\n   As $OB = x\\sqrt{2}$,\n   \\[\n   a \\sqrt{2} - x = x \\sqrt{2}\n   \\]\n\n8. **Solve for $x$:**\n\n   Rearrange the equation:\n   \\[\n   a\\sqrt{2} - x = x \\sqrt{2}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   a\\sqrt{2} = x \\sqrt{2} + x\n   \\]\n   Factor out $x$:\n   \\[\n   a\\sqrt{2} = x(\\sqrt{2} + 1)\n   \\]\n   Divide both sides by $\\sqrt{2} + 1$:\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{a \\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2} + 1}\n   \\]\n\n   Rationalize the denominator:\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{a \\sqrt{2} (\\sqrt{2} - 1)}{(\\sqrt{2} + 1)(\\sqrt{2} - 1)}\n   \\]\n   Simplify the denominator:\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{a \\sqrt{2} (\\sqrt{2} - 1)}{2 - 1} = a(2 - \\sqrt{2})\n   \\]\n\n9. **Conclusion:**\n\n   Thus, the radius of the circle is\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{a(2 - \\sqrt{2})}\n   \\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Equilateral triangles \\(A B C\\), \\(C D E\\), \\(E H K\\) (vertices are traversed counterclockwise) are arranged on the plane such that \\(\\overrightarrow{A D} = \\overrightarrow{D K}\\). Prove that triangle \\(B H D\\) is also equilateral.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to prove that triangle BHD is equilateral given these three equilateral triangles ABC, CDE, and EHK arranged such that the vector from A to D is equal to the vector from D to K. Hmm, let me start by visualizing the problem. Maybe drawing a diagram would help. Since all the given triangles are equilateral and arranged counterclockwise, their orientations are consistent. \n\nFirst, let me recall that in an equilateral triangle, all sides are equal and each angle is 60 degrees. Also, the problem mentions vectors: the vector from A to D is the same as the vector from D to K. That means AD and DK are equal in length and direction. So AD = DK, and they are parallel. So, point D is the midpoint between A and K? Wait, no. Because if vector AD = vector DK, that would mean that K is the translation of A by twice the vector AD. So, if you start at A, move along vector AD to get to D, then move the same vector again to get to K. So, K is such that D is the midpoint of A and K? Wait, actually, yes. Because if AD = DK in vector terms, then K = D + (D - A) = 2D - A. So, the coordinates of K would be 2D - A if we consider coordinate geometry. Maybe using coordinates would be a good approach here.\n\nLet me try setting up a coordinate system. Let's place point A at the origin (0, 0) for simplicity. Then, since ABC is an equilateral triangle, let's assign coordinates to B and C. Let's assume the side length of triangle ABC is 'a'. To make calculations easier, maybe take a = 1? Let me see. If ABC is equilateral with A at (0, 0), then point B can be at (1, 0). Point C would be at (0.5, (\u221a3)/2) because in an equilateral triangle, the height is (\u221a3)/2 times the side length. So, coordinates: A(0,0), B(1,0), C(0.5, \u221a3/2).\n\nNow, the next triangle is CDE. Since CDE is also equilateral and counterclockwise, the order of the vertices is C, D, E. So, starting at C, moving counterclockwise, so the next vertex is D, then E. Similarly, EHK is another equilateral triangle, counterclockwise, so E, H, K.\n\nGiven that vector AD = vector DK. Let me first find the coordinates of D and K based on this condition. Wait, but maybe we need to express D in terms of C first. Since CDE is an equilateral triangle, the position of D and E depends on the rotation from C. Since it's counterclockwise, rotating point C by 60 degrees around point D? Wait, no. Wait, the triangle is CDE, so the edges are CD, DE, EC. Since it's equilateral, CD = DE = EC. Wait, but the triangle is CDE, so starting at C, going to D, then to E, then back to C. So CD, DE, EC are the sides. So CD = DE = EC. So, CD is a side of the triangle, then DE, then EC.\n\nBut how is D positioned relative to C? Since ABC is already placed, we need to figure out the position of D. Maybe we need to use complex numbers for rotation. Let me recall that rotating a point by 60 degrees counterclockwise around another point can be achieved using complex numbers. If we have a point z, and we want to rotate it 60 degrees around point c, the formula is: (z - c) * e^(i\u03c0/3) + c. Since e^(i\u03c0/3) is cos(60\u00b0) + i sin(60\u00b0) = 0.5 + i (\u221a3)/2.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using vectors. Let me think. If CDE is an equilateral triangle, then vector DE can be obtained by rotating vector CD by 60 degrees counterclockwise. Similarly, vector EC is obtained by rotating vector DE by 60 degrees counterclockwise. Wait, maybe. Let me verify. If CDE is a counterclockwise equilateral triangle, then from point C to D to E, the turn from CD to DE is a 60-degree angle. So, to go from CD to DE, we rotate the vector CD by 60 degrees counterclockwise. So, vector DE = vector CD rotated by 60 degrees. Similarly, vector EC = vector DE rotated by 60 degrees.\n\nSimilarly, for triangle EHK, vector EK is obtained by rotating vector EH by 60 degrees counterclockwise.\n\nBut maybe this is getting a bit abstract. Let me try to assign coordinates step by step.\n\nStarting with ABC: A(0,0), B(1,0), C(0.5, \u221a3/2). Now, triangle CDE is next. Let's denote point D. Since CDE is an equilateral triangle, and it's counterclockwise. So starting at C, moving to D, then to E. The question is, where is D located? Since we need to maintain the equilateral triangle structure.\n\nAssuming that CD is a side of the triangle CDE. So, CD should have the same length as DE and EC. The direction of D from C is important. Since the triangle is counterclockwise, D should be such that when moving from C to D, then turning 60 degrees to the left (counterclockwise) gets us to E.\n\nAlternatively, using rotation. If we take point C and rotate it 60 degrees around point D to get E? Hmm, not sure. Wait, maybe we need to express point E in terms of point C and D. Let me think. If CDE is equilateral, then E can be obtained by rotating D around C by 60 degrees counterclockwise. Wait, that might be the case.\n\nLet me recall that in a counterclockwise equilateral triangle, each subsequent point is a 60-degree rotation from the previous side. So, from C to D, then from D to E, which is a 60-degree rotation from CD. Wait, perhaps using complex numbers would make this clearer.\n\nLet me model the points as complex numbers. Let me denote the complex plane with points A, B, C, D, E, H, K.\n\nLet me assign complex coordinates: Let A = 0, B = 1, C = 0.5 + i(\u221a3)/2. Then, triangle CDE is equilateral. Let me denote D as a complex number d, and E as e.\n\nSince CDE is equilateral counterclockwise, the rotation from CD to CE is 60 degrees. Wait, perhaps E is obtained by rotating vector CD by 60 degrees around point C. That is, E = C + (D - C) * e^(i\u03c0/3). Similarly, in complex numbers, multiplying by e^(i\u03c0/3) (which is 0.5 + i(\u221a3)/2) rotates a vector by 60 degrees.\n\nSo, E = C + (D - C) * (cos60\u00b0 + i sin60\u00b0). Therefore, E = C + (D - C) * (0.5 + i (\u221a3)/2).\n\nSimilarly, for triangle EHK, H and K are such that EHK is equilateral counterclockwise. So, similarly, K = E + (H - E) * e^(i\u03c0/3).\n\nBut we also have the condition that vector AD = vector DK. In complex numbers, vector AD is D - A = D - 0 = D. Vector DK is K - D. So the condition is D = K - D, which implies K = 2D.\n\nWait, that seems important. So if vector AD = vector DK, then D - A = K - D. Since A is at 0, this simplifies to D = K - D, so K = 2D. Therefore, K is twice the vector from the origin to D. So K = 2D. So if D is a complex number d, then K is 2d.\n\nBut we also need to express K in terms of E and H from the triangle EHK. So perhaps we can find expressions for E and H in terms of D, then use K = 2D to get a relationship.\n\nLet me try to proceed step by step.\n\nFirst, from triangle CDE:\n\nE = C + (D - C) * e^(i\u03c0/3). Let's compute e^(i\u03c0/3) = 0.5 + i (\u221a3)/2. Let's denote this as \u03c9 for simplicity. So \u03c9 = e^(i\u03c0/3).\n\nTherefore, E = C + (D - C)\u03c9.\n\nSimilarly, from triangle EHK:\n\nH is the next vertex after E in the equilateral triangle EHK. So starting at E, moving to H, then to K. So similar to CDE, we can write:\n\nK = E + (H - E)\u03c9.\n\nBut we also know that K = 2D. So substituting K:\n\n2D = E + (H - E)\u03c9.\n\nSo, 2D = E + H\u03c9 - E\u03c9.\n\nRearranging:\n\n2D = E(1 - \u03c9) + H\u03c9.\n\nBut we need to find expressions for E and H in terms of D. Let's first express E in terms of D from the CDE triangle.\n\nWe had E = C + (D - C)\u03c9.\n\nGiven that C is 0.5 + i(\u221a3)/2, substituting:\n\nE = (0.5 + i(\u221a3)/2) + (D - (0.5 + i(\u221a3)/2)) * \u03c9.\n\nLet me compute this. Let me denote D as a complex number d = x + iy.\n\nBut maybe instead of variables, let's try to express E in terms of D. Let me compute:\n\nE = C + (D - C)\u03c9 = C(1 - \u03c9) + D\u03c9.\n\nSimilarly, from triangle EHK, H can be expressed in terms of E and K. But K is 2D, so:\n\nK = E + (H - E)\u03c9 => 2D = E + H\u03c9 - E\u03c9 => H\u03c9 = 2D - E(1 - \u03c9) => H = [2D - E(1 - \u03c9)] / \u03c9.\n\nBut since we have E expressed in terms of D, we can substitute that into the equation for H.\n\nSo H = [2D - (C(1 - \u03c9) + D\u03c9)(1 - \u03c9)] / \u03c9.\n\nLet me compute this step by step.\n\nFirst, compute E(1 - \u03c9):\n\nE(1 - \u03c9) = [C(1 - \u03c9) + D\u03c9] (1 - \u03c9) = C(1 - \u03c9)^2 + D\u03c9(1 - \u03c9).\n\nTherefore,\n\nH = [2D - C(1 - \u03c9)^2 - D\u03c9(1 - \u03c9)] / \u03c9\n\n= [2D - D\u03c9(1 - \u03c9) - C(1 - \u03c9)^2] / \u03c9\n\nFactor out D:\n\n= [D(2 - \u03c9(1 - \u03c9)) - C(1 - \u03c9)^2] / \u03c9\n\nLet me compute 2 - \u03c9(1 - \u03c9). First, compute \u03c9(1 - \u03c9):\n\n\u03c9 = 0.5 + i(\u221a3)/2\n\n1 - \u03c9 = 1 - 0.5 - i(\u221a3)/2 = 0.5 - i(\u221a3)/2\n\nMultiply \u03c9(1 - \u03c9):\n\n(0.5 + i(\u221a3)/2)(0.5 - i(\u221a3)/2) = (0.5)^2 - (i(\u221a3)/2)^2 = 0.25 - ( -3/4 ) = 0.25 + 0.75 = 1\n\nSo \u03c9(1 - \u03c9) = 1. Therefore, 2 - \u03c9(1 - \u03c9) = 2 - 1 = 1.\n\nWow, that's a nice simplification. Therefore, the expression simplifies to:\n\nH = [D(1) - C(1 - \u03c9)^2] / \u03c9\n\nSo H = (D - C(1 - \u03c9)^2) / \u03c9\n\nNow, compute (1 - \u03c9)^2:\n\n1 - \u03c9 = 0.5 - i(\u221a3)/2\n\n(1 - \u03c9)^2 = (0.5)^2 - 2 * 0.5 * i(\u221a3)/2 + (i(\u221a3)/2)^2 = 0.25 - i(\u221a3)/2 + (-3/4) = (0.25 - 0.75) - i(\u221a3)/2 = (-0.5) - i(\u221a3)/2\n\nTherefore, C(1 - \u03c9)^2:\n\nC is 0.5 + i(\u221a3)/2. Multiply by (-0.5 - i(\u221a3)/2):\n\n(0.5)(-0.5) + (0.5)(-i\u221a3/2) + (i\u221a3/2)(-0.5) + (i\u221a3/2)(-i\u221a3/2)\n\nCompute each term:\n\nFirst term: 0.5 * -0.5 = -0.25\n\nSecond term: 0.5 * -i\u221a3/2 = -i\u221a3/4\n\nThird term: i\u221a3/2 * -0.5 = -i\u221a3/4\n\nFourth term: i\u221a3/2 * -i\u221a3/2 = -i\u00b2 * (3/4) = -(-1) * 3/4 = 3/4\n\nSo adding all terms:\n\n-0.25 - i\u221a3/4 - i\u221a3/4 + 3/4 = (-0.25 + 0.75) + (-i\u221a3/4 - i\u221a3/4) = 0.5 - i\u221a3/2\n\nTherefore, C(1 - \u03c9)^2 = 0.5 - i\u221a3/2\n\nTherefore, H = (D - (0.5 - i\u221a3/2)) / \u03c9\n\nBut D is a complex number, let's denote D as d. So:\n\nH = (d - (0.5 - i\u221a3/2)) / \u03c9\n\nNow, \u03c9 is 0.5 + i\u221a3/2, so dividing by \u03c9 is equivalent to multiplying by its reciprocal. Since \u03c9 is a complex number of unit magnitude (since |\u03c9| = sqrt(0.5^2 + (\u221a3/2)^2) = sqrt(0.25 + 0.75) = 1), its reciprocal is its conjugate, which is 0.5 - i\u221a3/2.\n\nTherefore, 1/\u03c9 = \u03c9\u0304 = 0.5 - i\u221a3/2.\n\nTherefore, H = [d - (0.5 - i\u221a3/2)] * (0.5 - i\u221a3/2)\n\nLet me compute this:\n\nFirst, compute [d - (0.5 - i\u221a3/2)] = d - 0.5 + i\u221a3/2\n\nLet me denote d as x + iy (but maybe keeping it symbolic is better). So:\n\nH = (d - 0.5 + i\u221a3/2)(0.5 - i\u221a3/2)\n\nMultiply these two complex numbers:\n\nFirst term: d * 0.5 = 0.5d\n\nSecond term: d * (-i\u221a3/2) = -i\u221a3/2 d\n\nThird term: -0.5 * 0.5 = -0.25\n\nFourth term: -0.5 * (-i\u221a3/2) = i\u221a3/4\n\nFifth term: i\u221a3/2 * 0.5 = i\u221a3/4\n\nSixth term: i\u221a3/2 * (-i\u221a3/2) = -i\u00b2 * 3/4 = 3/4\n\nSo combining all terms:\n\n0.5d - i\u221a3/2 d - 0.25 + i\u221a3/4 + i\u221a3/4 + 3/4\n\nSimplify:\n\n0.5d - i\u221a3/2 d + (-0.25 + 0.75) + (i\u221a3/4 + i\u221a3/4)\n\nWhich becomes:\n\n0.5d - i\u221a3/2 d + 0.5 + i\u221a3/2\n\nFactor out d:\n\nd(0.5 - i\u221a3/2) + 0.5 + i\u221a3/2\n\nBut notice that 0.5 - i\u221a3/2 is exactly \u03c9\u0304, which is 1/\u03c9. So:\n\nH = d * (1/\u03c9) + (0.5 + i\u221a3/2)\n\nBut 0.5 + i\u221a3/2 is exactly \u03c9. So:\n\nH = d / \u03c9 + \u03c9\n\nBut \u03c9 = e^(i\u03c0/3), so 1/\u03c9 = e^(-i\u03c0/3). Therefore, H = d e^(-i\u03c0/3) + e^(i\u03c0/3)\n\nHmm, interesting. So H is expressed in terms of d. B