or each valid configuration, derive constraints on x using the triangle inequality in the two faces that include x.\n\n3. **Case Analysis**:\n   - **Case 1**: Missing edge between vertices with edges assigned as AC=20, AD=70, BC=14, BD=40, CD=52:\n     - Faces ABC and ABD yield constraints: \\(30 < x < 34\\), leading to \\(x = 31, 32, 33\\).\n   - **Case 2**: Missing edge between vertices with edges assigned as AC=70, AD=20, BC=14, BD=40, CD=52:\n     - Faces ABC and ABD yield constraints: \\(56 < x < 60\\), leading to \\(x = 57, 58, 59\\).\n\n4. **Combine Results**:\n   - The valid integer values for x from both cases are \\(31, 32, 33, 57, 58, 59\\), totaling **6 distinct values**.\n\n### Conclusion:\nThe number of possible integer lengths for the sixth edge is **6**.\n\n\\boxed{E}", "ground_truth_solution": "\nTo solve the problem, we use the **Triangle Inequality Theorem**, which states that for any triangle with side lengths \\( a \\), \\( b \\), and \\( c \\), the following must be true:\n1. \\( a + b > c \\)\n2. \\( a + c > b \\)\n3. \\( b + c > a \\)\n\nBy combining these, for any three side lengths \\( a \\), \\( b \\), and \\( c \\) (with \\( a \\leq b \\leq c \\)), it suffices to verify \\( a + b > c \\) since \\( a \\leq b \\leq c \\) implies the other inequalities are automatically satisfied.\n\nGiven five edge lengths of a tetrahedron: 14, 20, 40, 52, and 70. We need to find the possible sixth edge lengths.\n\nLet the sixth edge length be \\( x \\).\n\nWe'll treat the tetrahedron as having vertices \\( A, B, C, \\) and \\( D \\), with \\( BC = 70 \\). Now, \\( BC \\) is an edge in two triangles, \\( \\triangle ABC \\) and \\( \\triangle BDC \\).\n\n1. We find possible edges \\( AB \\), \\( AC \\), \\( AD \\), \\( BD \\), and \\( DC \\).\n\nLet's examine the possibility using \\( AC = 52 \\).\n\n### Case 1: \\( AB = 40 \\)\n\nWe analyze the remaining edges, 14, 20, and \\( x \\).\n\n1. If \\( AB = 40 \\), the remaining triangles \\( \\triangle ABD \\) and \\( \\triangle ADC \\) are checked for feasible compositions using 14, 20, and the unknown length \\( x \\):\n\n\\[ AB + BD > AD \\rightarrow 40 + BD > AD \\]\n\\[ AB + AD > BD \\rightarrow 40 + AD > BD \\]\n\nAdditionally,\n\\[ AC + BC > 70 \\rightarrow 52 + 70 > 70 \\quad \\text{(This is always true since } AC \\text{ is already known)} \\].\n\n### Case 2: \\( AB = 20 \\)\n\nNext, examine the edge compositions 14, 40, and \\( x \\):\n\nConsidering \\( AD = 14 \\) and \\( DC = 40 \\):\n\nFrom \\( \\triangle ABD \\):\n\\[ 20 + 14 > x \\rightarrow x < 34 \\]\n\n\\[ 14 + x > 20 \\rightarrow x > 6 \\]\n\nFrom \\( \\triangle BDC \\):\n\\[ 40 + 70 > x \\rightarrow x < 110 \\]\n\\[ 70 + x > 40 \\rightarrow x > 30 \\]\n\nCombining these:\n\\[ 31 \\leq x \\leq 33 \\]\n\nConsidering \\( DC = 14 \\) and \\( AD = 40 \\):\n\nFrom \\( \\triangle ABD \\):\n\\[ 20 + 40 > x \\rightarrow x < 60 \\]\n\n\\[ 20 + x > 40 \\rightarrow x > 20 \\]\n\nFrom \\( \\triangle BDC \\):\n\\[ 14 + 70 > x \\rightarrow x < 84 \\]\n\n\\[ 14 + x > 70 \\rightarrow x > 56 \\]\n\nCombining these:\n\\[ 57 \\leq x \\leq 59 \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\n\nCombining all possible ranges:\n- \\( 31 \\leq x \\leq 33 \\)\n- \\( 57 \\leq x \\leq 59 \\)\n\nThus, \\( x \\) can take 6 distinct values: 31, 32, 33, 57, 58, 59.\n\nTherefore, the number of possible lengths for the sixth edge is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{6}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Calculate the numbers \\( p \\) and \\( q \\) such that the roots of the equation\n\n\\[ x^2 + p x + q = 0 \\]\n\nare \\( D \\) and \\( 1 - D \\), where \\( D \\) is the discriminant of this quadratic equation.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let's see. I need to find the values of p and q such that the quadratic equation x\u00b2 + px + q = 0 has roots D and 1 - D, where D is the discriminant of the equation itself. Hmm, this sounds a bit circular because the discriminant depends on p and q, which are the variabl